window.CM_DECKS = {"1":{"pages":{"1":{"t":"Day 1","s":"Case Management","w":"Case Management Training Day 1"},"2":{"t":"Training Agenda","s":"Training Agenda","w":"Understand Case Management Fundamentals Intake, Case Evaluation, and Representation Approval Case Phase : Treatment 02 Training Agenda 01 02 03"},"3":{"t":"What is Case Management?","s":"Case Management Fundamentals","w":"Case Management Fundamentals What is Case Management? Case Management is the end-to-end coordination, tracking, and handling of a case from intake to resolution. It ensures: Timely progression of cases Proper documentation and compliance Effective communication between all parties"},"4":{"t":"The General Process","s":"Case Management Fundamentals","w":"Case Management Fundamentals The General Process"},"5":{"t":"Key Duties","s":"Case Management Fundamentals","w":"DRAFT Resource Allocation Case Management Fundamentals Key Duties Relevant Laws and Legal Compliance Research Client Communication Case Progress Monitoring Case Updates and Summaries Task Compliance Monitoring Planning and Recommendation Legal Document Drafting and Filing Case Health Analysis"},"6":{"t":"Intake Phase Investigation Phase / Claim Set Up","s":"Understanding Case Phases","w":"Understanding Case Phases Case Management is divided into structured phases that guide how a case is handled from start to finish. Each phase has specific goals, responsibilities, and deliverables that ensure the case progresses efficiently and accurately. Intake Phase Investigation Phase / Claim Set Up Treatment Phase Pre Demand Settlement and Lien Negotiations Disbursement Litigation Demand"},"7":{"t":"Case Phase : Intake","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Case Phase : Intake The Intake Phase is where Case Managers establish control, structure, and clarity from the very beginning of a case. This is not just data collection—it is the first opportunity to ensure accuracy, compliance, and proper case direction. As a Case Manager, your role in intake is to verify, and organize essential case information while ensuring the case meets acceptance standards. You must understand that intake is responsible for: Capturing complete and accurate client details Screening for conflicts of interest Assessing case viability and eligibility Establishing a properly structured case file 👉 This phase determines whether the case should move forward or be declined."},"8":{"t":"Case Phase : Intake","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Case Phase : Intake In a real intake workflow, a Case Manager will: Speak with or review client-submitted information Ask structured intake questions (who, what, when, where, how) Run a conflict check in the system before proceeding Review documents for completeness and relevance Create a case file with proper naming conventions and system entry Example Workflow: Example Workflow: Client submits inquiry form Case Manager reviews and verifies details Conflict check is completed Case is approved or escalated for review Case file is created and logged in the system Example Workflow: Client submits inquiry form Case Manager reviews and verifies details Conflict check is completed Case is approved or escalated for review Case file is created and logged in the system As a Case Manager, you should work independently to: As a Case Manager, you should work independently to: Conduct a full intake call or review intake submission Identify missing or inconsistent client information Perform and document a conflict check accurately Decide if the case meets basic acceptance criteria Set up a complete and organized case file in the system Set up a complete and organized case file in the system Communicate next steps clearly to the client or internal team Conduct a full intake call or review intake submission Identify missing or inconsistent client information Perform and document a conflict check accurately Decide if the case meets basic acceptance criteria Set up a complete and organized case file in the system Communicate next steps clearly to the client or internal team"},"9":{"t":"Intake and Initial Client Contact","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Intake and Initial Client Contact Initial client contact is the first interaction with a potential client, followed immediately by intake. It is used to gather basic facts, assess urgency, and determine if the case can move forward. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake The Case Manager’s role begins after intake is completed, functioning as a quality control and case validation reviewer before a case is officially accepted into active case management. 👉 Case Managers are the gatekeepers of case quality. Their responsibility is to ensure that intake work is: Complete Accurate Properly documented Ready for case activation"},"10":{"t":"Case Acceptance Determination","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Case Acceptance Determination After the Case Manager completes the verification call and confirms the facts of loss, the case moves into a review and evaluation stage for acceptance. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. At this point, the Case Manager is no longer gathering information—they are assessing whether the firm will accept the case based on verified facts and intake completeness. 👉 This step ensures the case is factually confirmed, properly documented, and aligned with firm criteria before acceptance. Case Phase : Intake"},"11":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Intake bottlenecks are points in the intake process where work slows down, gets stuck, or becomes inconsistent, delaying case progression. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. These bottlenecks usually occur due to: Case Phase : Intake Missing or incomplete information Delayed client responses Poorly documented intake submissions Verification or conflict check delays Unclear case eligibility decisions"},"12":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Incomplete Client Information Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake Missing contact details or incident facts No supporting documents attached Vague or unclear case summaries"},"13":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Delayed Client Follow-Up Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake Clients not responding to clarification requests Missing verification call confirmations Slow submission of required documents"},"14":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Conflict Check Delays Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake System backlog or manual verification delays Incomplete conflict check entries Unclear opposing party information"},"15":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Intake Form Errors Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake Incorrect dates or inconsistent statements Missing signatures or authorization forms Duplicate or misfiled records"},"16":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Case Eligibility Uncertainty Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake submitted without clear legal assessment Requires attorney review before decision Ambiguous facts of loss or liability"},"17":{"t":"Tools for Case Planning","s":"Case Phases","w":"Phase Strategic Focus Key Case Manager Tasks Intake & Investigation Liability Confirm insurance coverage (PIP/MedPay, BI, UM/UIM); Order police reports and 911 transcripts; Identify all potential defendants. Treatment (Med-Pay) Damages Track \"Client Compliance.\" Ensure no gaps in treatment. Gather diagnostic imaging (MRI/CT) results immediately to \"objective-ize\" the injury. Demand Phase Value Maximization Compile a \"Special Damages\" summary (Medical bills + Lost wages). Request \"Final Narratives\" from surgeons to establish permanent impairment. Litigation Evidence Preservation Manage the discovery calendar; Coordinate depositions; Facilitate expert witness (accident recon/medical) reviews. The PI Case Lifecycle Strategy Case Phases Tools for Case Planning ICase Management Software Task Trackers / Calendars Document Management Systems Workflow Automation Tools System = Efficiency + Accountabilit When working within a legal framework, Case Management shifts from \"best efforts\" to \"strict \"best efforts\" to \"strict compliance.\" compliance.\" When working within a legal framework, Case Management shifts from \"best efforts\" to \"strict compliance.\" A PI case is a race against the Statute of Limitations and the \"gap in treatment\" defense used by insurance companies."},"18":{"t":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","s":"Case Phase : Intake","w":"Common Intake Bottlenecks Communication Gaps Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. a settlement. Every great legal outcome starts long before we enter a courtroom or sign a settlement. Case Phase : Intake team not updating Case Managers Case Manager not receiving complete intake packet Miscommunication between departments"},"19":{"t":"“INTAKE DECISION CHALLENGE” “ INTAKE DECISION CHALLENGE ”","s":"Skill Building : Case Phase - Intake","w":"Skill Building : Case Phase - Intake A new potential client, John Doe, submits an inquiry regarding a case against Apex Delivery Services. A new potential client, John Doe, submits an inquiry regarding a case against Apex Delivery Services. Your task is to complete the intake evaluation process and determine whether the case should be Your task is to complete the intake evaluation process and determine whether the case should be accepted, rejected, or escalated for review. accepted, rejected, or escalated for review. Clearly document the reason for the decision Communicate next steps to intake team or client A new potential client, John Doe, submits an inquiry regarding a case against Apex Delivery Services. Your task is to complete the intake evaluation process and determine whether the case should be accepted, rejected, or escalated for review. Clearly document the reason for the decision Communicate next steps to intake team or client “INTAKE DECISION CHALLENGE” “INTAKE DECISION CHALLENGE” ✅ Accepted for case opening ⚠ Escalated for attorney/senior review ❌ Dec Declined due to eligibility or risk factors lined due to eligibility or risk factors ✅ Accepted for case opening ⚠️ Escalated for attorney/senior review ❌ Declined due to eligibility or risk factors"},"20":{"t":"APPLIED CASE MANAGER ACTIONS","s":"Skill Building : Case Phase - Intake","w":"Skill Building : Case Phase - Intake Identify where intake is breaking down and what actions should be taken to move the file forward. Identify where intake is breaking down and what actions should be taken to move the file forward. Identify where intake is breaking down and what actions should be taken to move the file forward. APPLIED CASE MANAGER ACTIONS Identify areas of slowed intake Conduct root cause analysis Implement immediate actions Prevent future occurrences Identify areas of slowed intake Conduct root cause analysis Implement immediate actions Prevent future occurrences"},"21":{"t":"Coverage Determination and Liability","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Early Evaluation Phase Case Phases: Claim Set Up Coverage Determination and Liability Coverage Determination Coverage determination is the process of identifying which insurance policies apply to a claim and what benefits, limits, and exclusions exist. Is there valid insurance coverage? What type of coverage applies? What are the policy limits? Are there exclusions or coverage gaps? Liability Assessment Liability assessment determines who is legally responsible for the incident and to what degree. Who caused the incident? Is liability clear, shared, or disputed? What evidence supports fault? Are there comparative negligence factors?"},"22":{"t":"Coverage Determination and Liability","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Early Evaluation Phase Case Phases: Claim Set Up Coverage Determination and Liability Case Projection Case projection is the early estimation of case value, risk, and outcome trajectory based on: Coverage availability Liability strength Injury severity Treatment expectations Documentation quality Why These Three Work Together You cannot accurately project a case unless you understand: 🛡️ Coverage = “Is there money to pay the claim?” ⚖️ Liability = “Who is responsible?” 📈 Projection = “What is this case likely worth and how will it move?”"},"23":{"t":"Coverage Determination and Liability","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Coverage Type Status Notes Apex Commercial Auto Liability Active (assumed) Primary coverage source Employer Umbrella Unknown Must verify additional Client PIP / MedPay Unknown Requires insurance UM/UIM Coverage Pending Depends on Apex policy Case: John Doe v. Apex Logistics Case Phases: Claim Set Up Coverage Determination and Liability Case Snapshot Client: John Doe Defendant: Apex Logistics (commercial trucking company) Incident Type: Motor vehicle collision (rear-end involving commercial vehicle) Location: Highway traffic slowdown collision Date of Loss: Recent intake (pending verification) 🔍 Identified Potential Coverage Sources Coverage Analysis Summary Apex Logistics is a commercial carrier → high likelihood of substantial liability coverage Policy limits are expected to be higher than standard auto policies 🧠 Case Manager Insight: Coverage appears strong on defendant side, but confirmation is required before projection can be finalized."},"24":{"t":"Coverage Determination and Liability","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Factor Observation Accident Type Rear-end collision involving commercial vehicle Road Conditions Traffic slowdown Client Position Struck from behind Defendant Conduct Possible following too closely / failure to brake Evidence No police report yet Case: John Doe v. Apex Logistics Case Phases: Claim Set Up Coverage Determination and Liability Determination Fact Pattern Analysis Liability Assessment Rear-end collisions involving commercial vehicles generally create a strong presumption of negligence. Apex driver may be liable for: Negligent operation Failure to maintain safe distance Employer vicarious liability ⚠️ Risk Note: Liability is strong but not final until confirmed by: Police report Driver statement Dashcam or other evidence 🧠 Insight: Liability is likely favorable, but still in pre-confirmation stage."},"25":{"t":"Case Planning Framework","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Regulatory Intake & Verification Case Planning Framework Before clinical planning begins, you must clear the \"Regulatory Triple-Check\" to ensure the claim is properly established and the case manager is authorized to act. HIPAA Compliance Obtain a claim-specific Authorization to Release Medical Information, as standard releases may not be adequate for personal injury cases. It should specify the incident date and permit communication with legal and insurance parties. Medicare/Medicaid Reporting Subrogation Management ***MMSEA - Medicare, Medicaid, and SCHIP Extension Act of 2007 If a client is 65+ or on SSDI, report the case to the Benefits Coordination & Recovery Center (BCRC) to avoid severe federal penalties and potential settlement freezes. Primary Payer Determination: Identify the \"Payor Order\" (e.g., Personal Injury Protection (PIP) / MedPay $\\rightarrow$ Private Health Insurance $\\rightarrow$ LOP/Provider Liens). Case Phases: Claim Set Up"},"26":{"t":"Case Planning Framework","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"The Three-Pillar Case Plan Case Planning Framework Once the claim is set up, the plan must be filed (often within 30–60 days depending on the state). It should be structured into three distinct categories: Clinical Acute interventions (PT, Specialty consults). Functional Subrogation Management ***MMSEA - Medicare, Medicaid, and SCHIP Extension Act of 2007 ADLs and \"Return to Work\" (RTW) status. Primary Payer Determination: Identify the \"Payor Order\" (e.g., Personal Injury Protection (PIP) / MedPay $\\rightarrow$ Private Health Insurance $\\rightarrow$ LOP/Provider Liens). Case Phases: Claim Set Up"},"27":{"t":"Case Planning Framework","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"Mandatory Disclosure & Compliance Tracking Case Planning Framework US Case Managers have a duty to maintain a \"clean\" file that is discoverable in litigation. Electronic Record Integrity Ensure your Case Management System (CMS) is SOC2 compliant and maintains an unalterable audit trail. State-Specific Timelines Letter of Protection (LOP) Management Example: In \"No-Fault\" states (like NY or FL), the framework must include a timeline for IME (Independent Medical Examinations) which the carrier will mandate early in the set-up. If the client is uninsured, the plan must track providers working under an LOP to avoid \"surprise\" medical bills that exceed the policy limits. Case Phases: Claim Set Up"},"28":{"t":"Case Planning Framework","s":"Case Phases: Claim Set Up","w":"The \"Golden Rule\" of US Claim Set-up Case Planning Framework US Case Managers have a duty to maintain a \"clean\" file that is discoverable in litigation. Coordinate, don't just Commingle. Your framework must treat the medical plan and the legal claim as two parallel tracks that never cross-contaminate. Avoid offering legal opinions in medical notes, as these are often used by defense counsel to impeach the case manager's neutrality. Case Phases: Claim Set Up"},"29":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"During this phase, your primary focus shifts from \"What do they need?\" to \"Is the plan working?\" During this phase, your primary focus shifts from \"What do they need?\" to \"Is the plan working?\" During this phase, your primary focus shifts from \"What do they need?\" to \"Is the plan working?\" Your role is built on three pillars: Your role is built on three pillars: During this phase, your primary focus shifts from \"What do they need?\" to \"Is the plan working?\" Your role is built on three pillars: Facilitation The Treatment Phase This phase is the “Engine Room” of Case Management for achieving set goals. CMs must balance support, monitoring, achieving set goals. CMs must balance support, monitoring, and problem-solving. and problem-solving. This phase is the “Engine Room” of Case Management for achieving set goals. CMs must balance support, monitoring, and problem-solving. Core Objectives Ensuring the client can actually access the services (e.g., transportation, paperwork, scheduling). Ensuring the client can actually access the services (e.g., transportation, paperwork, scheduling). Ensuring the client can actually access the services (e.g., transportation, paperwork, scheduling). Ensuring the client can actually access the services (e.g., transportation, paperwork, scheduling). Coordination Making sure the therapist, the doctor, and the social worker aren't working at cross-purposes. Making sure the therapist, the doctor, and the social worker aren't working at cross-purposes. Making sure the therapist, the doctor, and the social worker aren't working at cross-purposes. Maki"},"30":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Monitoring Progress The Treatment Phase Key Responsibilities & Skills You aren't just checking boxes; you are evaluating clinical and functional outcomes. You aren't just checking boxes; you are evaluating clinical and functional outcomes. You aren't just checking boxes; you are evaluating clinical and functional outcomes. You aren't just checking boxes; you are evaluating clinical and functional outcomes. Identifying Barriers Treatment rarely goes perfectly. Case managers must be \"barrier detectives.\" Treatment rarely goes perfectly. Case managers must be \"barrier detectives.\" Crisis Intervention Intervening when a client faces service denial or bureaucratic obstacles. Intervening when a client faces service denial or bureaucratic obstacles. Client Check-in: Ask specific questions about the treatment plan, like therapy session Client Check-in: Ask specific questions about the treatment plan, like therapy session Client Check-in: Ask specific questions about the treatment plan, like therapy session attendance, instead of just \"How are you?\" attendance, instead of just \"How are you?\" Documentation: Utilize \"GIRP\" or \"SOAP\" notes to track behavioral changes and milestones. Documentation: Utilize \"GIRP\" or \"SOAP\" notes to track behavioral changes and milestones. Documentation: Utilize \"GIRP\" or \"SOAP\" notes to track behavioral changes and milestones. Client Check-in: Ask specific questions about the treatment plan, like therapy session attendance, instead of just \"How are you?\" Documentation: Utilize \"GIRP\" or \"SOAP\" notes to track behavioral changes and milestones. Internal B"},"31":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Assess Response Evaluate Efficacy Adjust Intervention Motivational Interviewing The Treatment Phase Essential Client Communication Strategies Helps clients stay committed when the \"newness\" of treatment wears off. wears off. Helps clients stay committed when the \"newness\" of treatment wears off. Boundaries Prevents burnout and keeps the focus on client self-sufficiency. Prevents burnout and keeps the focus on client self-sufficiency. Active Listening Ensures the client feels heard and reduces misunderstandings with providers. Ensures the client feels heard and reduces misunderstandings with providers. The Treatment Phase is not linear. It is a cycle. Case managers should regularly use The Treatment Phase is not linear. It is a cycle. Case managers should regularly use The Treatment Phase is not linear. It is a cycle. Case managers should regularly use the following formula to determine if a plan needs a \"course correction\": the following formula to determine if a plan needs a \"course correction\": the following formula to determine if a plan needs a \"course correction\": The Treatment Phase is not linear. It is a cycle. Case managers should regularly use the following formula to determine if a plan needs a \"course correction\": ***If a client isn't achieving their goals, it's usually due to the plan rather than the individual. ***If a client isn't achieving their goals, it's usually due to the plan rather than the individual. ***If a client isn't achieving their goals, it's usually due to the plan rather than the individual. Modifying the plan reflects proactive management, no"},"32":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Technique How to Apply it in Treatment Developing Discrepancy Help the client see the gap between their current behavior and their long-term goals. Rolling with Resistance If a client refuses a service, don't argue. Pivot. \"It sounds like that specific clinic isn't a good fit for you. Let's look at why.\" OARS Use Open-ended questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, and Summaries. Goal: What part of the treatment plan are we addressing today? plan are we addressing today? Goal: What part of the treatment plan are we addressing today? Clinical Documentation The Treatment Phase Handling the Treatment Phase Effectively Your notes serve as the legal and professional record of treatment success. Your notes serve as the legal and professional record of treatment success. Your notes serve as the legal and professional record of treatment success. Your notes serve as the legal and professional record of treatment success. Motivational Interviewing At the midpoint of treatment, many clients experience a plateau as initial At the midpoint of treatment, many clients experience a plateau as initial At the midpoint of treatment, many clients experience a plateau as initial excitement wanes and hard work begins. excitement wanes and hard work begins. At the midpoint of treatment, many clients experience a plateau as initial excitement wanes and hard work begins. Intervention: What did you do? Intervention: What did you do? (e.g., \"Provided psychoeducation (e.g., \"Provided psychoeducation on medication side effects.\") on medication side effects.\") Intervention: What did you do? (e.g.,"},"33":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Instead of saying, \"Here is the number for the housing office,\" a high-skill case manager says, \"I know Sarah at the housing office. Let's call Instead of saying, \"Here is the number for the housing office,\" a high-skill case manager says, \"I know Sarah at the housing office. Let's call Instead of saying, \"Here is the number for the housing office,\" a high-skill case manager says, \"I know Sarah at the housing office. Let's call her together right now to schedule your intake.\" This reduces the \"referral leak\" where clients drop out of treatment during transitions. her together right now to schedule your intake.\" This reduces the \"referral leak\" where clients drop out of treatment during transitions. her together right now to schedule your intake.\" This reduces the \"referral leak\" where clients drop out of treatment during transitions. Instead of saying, \"Here is the number for the housing office,\" a high-skill case manager says, \"I know Sarah at the housing office. Let's call her together right now to schedule your intake.\" This reduces the \"referral leak\" where clients drop out of treatment during transitions. Proactive Resource Coordination The Treatment Phase Handling the Treatment Phase Effectively You are the \"hub\" of the wheel. To handle this phase effectively, you must master You are the \"hub\" of the wheel. To handle this phase effectively, you must master You are the \"hub\" of the wheel. To handle this phase effectively, you must master the Warm Handoff. the Warm Handoff. You are the \"hub\" of the wheel. To handle this phase effectively, you must master the Warm Handof"},"34":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The Treatment Phase Red Flags As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. Physical Therapy & Conservative Care In legal cases, PT is often the \"basel In legal cases, PT is often the \"baseline\" ine\" ine\" of treatment. Red flags here usually signal that the case is stagnating or being \"over-treated\" for billing purposes. of treatment. Red flags here usually signal that the case is stagnating or being \"over-treated\" for billing purposes. of treatment. Red flags here usually signal that the case is stagnating or being \"over-treated\" for billing purposes. In legal cases, PT is often the \"baseline\" of treatment. Red flags here usually signal that the case is stagnating or being \"over-treated\" for billing purposes. The client has attended over 12 sessions with no improvement in Range of Motion (ROM) or pain, yet the therapist hasn't suggested a specialist or imaging. The client has attended over 12 sessions with no improvement in Range of Motion (ROM) or pain, yet the therapist hasn't suggested a specialist or imaging. The client has attended over 12 sessions with no improvement in Range of Motion (ROM) or pain, yet the therapist hasn't suggested a specialist or imaging. The client has attended over 12 sessions with no "},"35":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The Treatment Phase Red Flags As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. As a Case Manager, it is essential to become proficient in the standard treatment protocols in the US. Pain Management & Medication A client is prescribed an opioid, a muscle relaxant, and a sedative (The \"Holy Trinity\"). This is a massive safety risk and a legal liability for \"contributory negligence\" A client is prescribed an opioid, a muscle relaxant, and a sedative (The \"Holy Trinity\"). This is a massive safety risk and a legal liability for \"contributory negligence\" A client is prescribed an opioid, a muscle relaxant, and a sedative (The \"Holy Trinity\"). This is a massive safety risk and a legal liability for \"contributory negligence\" if the client has an accident while medicated. if the client has an accident while medicated. A client is prescribed an opioid, a muscle relaxant, and a sedative (The \"Holy Trinity\"). This is a massive safety risk and a legal liability for \"contributory negligence\" if the client has an accident while medicated. The Urine Drug Screen (UDS) shows the client is not taking their prescribed medication (indicates possible diversion/selling) or shows illicit substances. The Urine Drug Screen (UDS) shows the client is not taking their prescribed medication (indicates possible diversion/selling) or shows illicit su"},"36":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The Assessment Phase Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment Before a plan can be built, you need a comprehensive understanding of the \"whole person.\" This involves more than just reviewing medical records. Clinical Evaluation Identifying physical, cognitive, and psychological impairments. Social Determinants Pre-Morbid Status Assessing housing, family support systems, and financial stability. Establishing the client's \"baseline\" to differentiate between pre- existing conditions and accident-related injuries. Vocational/Educational Outlook Determining if the client can return to their previous role or requires retraining."},"37":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Goal Attribute Application in Case Management Specific Instead of \"improve mobility,\" use \"ambulate 50 meters with a rolling walker.\" Measurable Use standardized scales (e.g., FIM scores, PHQ-9 for depression). Achievable Ensure the goal is realistic based on the prognosis. Relevant The goal must relate directly to the injuries sustained in the claim. Time-bound Set clear milestones (e.g., \"by the 12-week review\"). SMART Goal Setting Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment In a personal injury context, goals must be defensible and measurable to satisfy both clinicians and legal adjusters."},"38":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The Care Coordination Strategy Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment This is where you map out the specific interventions required. A standard framework categorizes these into: Acute/Restorative Care: Surgery, Physiotherapy, and Occupational Therapy. Psychological Support: Counseling or CBT to address PTSD, anxiety, or adjustment disorders. Durable Medical Equipment (DME): Sourcing wheelchairs, ramps, or assistive technology. Rehabilitation Milestones: Transitioning from inpatient to outpatient or home-based care."},"39":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"EMC Chiropractic Care Pain Management Ortho The Standard Treatment Map Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment Emergency Medical Services Emergency Room Treatment MRI/ Radiology Physical Therapy Surgery Long Term Pain Management Post-Operative Care"},"40":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Phase Timeline Typical Interventions Case Management Focus Acute Days 1–14 ER, Imaging (MRI/CT), bracing, RICE, initial PT. Establishing Causation and \"MedPay\" coverage. Sub-Acute Weeks 2–8 Active Physical Therapy (2–3x weekly), Specialist f/up. Monitoring Functional Gains (e.g., ROM, weight-bearing). Restorative Months 2–6 Work Hardening, Injections, Psych adjustment. Identifying Barriers to RTW (Return to Work). Maintenance 6 Months+ Home Exercise Program (HEP), PRN visits. Determining MMI (Maximum Medical Improvement). Treatment Road Map Mastery Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment Mastering the Standard Treatment Map is the difference between a Case Manager who simply \"observes\" a recovery and one who \"drives\" it. In the US personal injury landscape, deviations from these maps are the primary reason for insurance denials and \"IME\" (Independent Medical Examination) triggers."},"41":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Variance Analysis: Why the Map Fails Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment When a client drifts off the map, the case manager must diagnose the \"Why\" before the insurer terminates funding. Clinical Variance Psychosocial Variance Secondary complications (e.g., a patient with a back injury develops radiculopathy/nerve pain that wasn't in the initial ER report). \"Yellow Flags\" such as fear-avoidance behavior, depression, or lack of transportation to therapy. Systemic Variance Delays in insurance authorization for an MRI or specialist referral."},"42":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Communication Loops: The \"Stakeholder Triad\" Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment In the US, the Case Manager acts as the central router for information. A breakdown in any of these loops can stall a case for months. The Clinical Loop (MDT) The Legal Loop (Solicitor/Attorney) Ensure the Physical Therapist's notes align with the Orthopedist's orders. If the PT says \"Patient is 90% recovered\" but the Ortho says \"Total Disability,\" the case has a documentation crisis. Providing \"Status Reports\" that translate medical jargon into functional evidence. They need to know what the client can't do (ADLs) to calculate \"Pain and Suffering\" damages. The Financial Loop (Insurer/Adjuster) Proactive updates before the 6-week \"Physician Referral\" expires. This prevents gaps in treatment that the defense can later frame as \"non-compliance.\""},"43":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The \"Golden Standard\": Reasonable and Necessary Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment Under US law, specifically in the context of Utilization Review (UR), every item in your monitoring framework must meet these two criteria: Reasonable Necessary Is the treatment common for this specific MOI (Mechanism of Injury)? Is there objective evidence (not just subjective pain) that the treatment is improving function? The Red Flag Rule If a client shows no change in their Oswestry Disability Index (ODI) score after 12 weeks of Physical Therapy, the \"Treatment Map\" indicates a plateau. A proactive case manager should seek a specialist or a second opinion instead of persisting with ineffective treatment."},"44":{"t":"The Treatment Phase","s":"Case Phases","w":"Case Phases The Treatment Phase Immediate Action Protocol When you spot a red flag, follow this A-C-T protocol: When you spot a red flag, follow this A-C-T protocol: Assess Is th Is the flag a Medical Danger (fever, hardware failure) or a Legal Danger e flag a Medical Danger (fever, hardware failure) or a Legal Danger e flag a Medical Danger (fever, hardware failure) or a Legal Danger (gaps in care, generic notes)? (gaps in care, generic notes)? Is the flag a Medical Danger (fever, hardware failure) or a Legal Danger (gaps in care, generic notes)? Clarify Call the provider’s Medical Assistant. Ask: \"The client has had 15 PT sessions Call the provider’s Medical Assistant. Ask: \"The client has had 15 PT sessions Call the provider’s Medical Assistant. Ask: \"The client has had 15 PT sessions with no progress; is the doctor considering an Orthopedic referral?\" with no progress; is the doctor considering an Orthopedic referral?\" Call the provider’s Medical Assistant. Ask: \"The client has had 15 PT sessions with no progress; is the doctor considering an Orthopedic referral?\" Track Document the red flag and your attempt to fix it. If the attorney needs to \"drop\" a Document the red flag and your attempt to fix it. If the attorney needs to \"drop\" a Document the red flag and your attempt to fix it. If the attorney needs to \"drop\" a provider because they are damaging the case, your documentation provides the provider because they are damaging the case, your documentation provides the provider because they are damaging the case, your documentation provides the \"why.\" \"why.\" \"why.\" Docum"},"45":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Illustration Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment Under US law, specifically in the context of Utilization Review (UR), every item in your monitoring framework must meet these two criteria:"},"46":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Metric Purpose When to Use Oswestry/DASH Measures ADL disability. Every 30 days. Gait Analysis Measures mobility/fall risk. For lower extremity injuries. PHQ-9 / GAD-7 Screens for Depression/Anxiety. If physical progress stalls (Yellow Flags). FCE Functional Capacity Evaluation. Before \"Return to Work\" or at MMI. How to use this Map for \"Course Correction\" Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment 1. Identifying the \"Plateau\" (The 6-Week Rule) Most adjusters look for a \"Functional Gap\" at the 6-week mark. The Map Expectation: A 20–30% improvement in Range of Motion (ROM) or a reduction in pain scores (VAS). The Variance: If the client is still at a 10/10 pain level after 12 sessions of PT, the map tells you to pivot immediately. Do not wait for the adjuster to deny the next 6 sessions. 2. Monitoring via Objective Metrics To keep the communication loop \"tight\" with the insurer, your monitoring notes should use these standard US tools:"},"47":{"t":"Case Planning - Treatment","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"The \"Yellow Flag\" Warning System Case Phases - Treatment Case Planning - Treatment As a Case Manager, you are monitoring for psychosocial barriers that \"derail\" the map: Catastrophizing: \"I will never walk again\" (despite minor clinical findings). Litigation Stress: High conflict with the defense side slowing recovery. Iatrogenic Factors: Dependency on opioids or excessive \"passive\" care (ice/heat) vs. \"active\" care (exercise). When you communicate with the Solicitor (Attorney) and Insurer, use the map to justify your plan: Client is currently in Phase 2 (Sub-Acute). We have noted a Clinical Variance: physical therapy has not reduced radicular pain. Per the Standard Treatment Map, I am recommending an MRI and Neurosurgical consult to rule out surgical necessity before proceeding to Phase 3. This ensures treatment remains Reasonable and Necessary.\""},"48":{"t":"Treatment Gaps","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Handling Treatment Gaps During the Treatment Phase Case Phases - Treatment Gaps 🎯 Why Early Intervention Matters Treatment gaps identified during active care can: Weaken injury credibility Delay recovery progress Create documentation inconsistencies Reduce future settlement value 👉 Early case management intervention helps prevent larger demand-phase issues later. 🔍 Common Causes of Treatment Gaps Missed appointments Transportation problems Financial concerns or co-pays Work schedule conflicts Client stops treatment after temporary symptom relief Delays in referrals, imaging, or authorizations"},"49":{"t":"Treatment Gaps","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Handling Treatment Gaps During the Treatment Phase Case Phases - Treatment Gaps 🛠 Case Manager Responsibilities ✅ Monitor treatment frequency and appointment compliance ✅ Follow up immediately after missed appointments ✅ Educate clients on the importance of consistent treatment ✅ Help coordinate transportation, scheduling, or referrals ✅ Communicate with providers regarding care interruptions ✅ Document all client explanations and follow-up efforts 📋 Best Practice During Active Treatment Maintain updated treatment timelines Track referral completion and pending appointments Encourage clients to report worsening symptoms promptly Escalate prolonged or unexplained gaps to supervising staff or attorneys 👉 Consistent treatment documentation strengthens both medical support and future negotiation leverage."},"50":{"t":"Common Bottlenecks & Challenges","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Case Phases - Treatment Common Bottlenecks & Challenges Escalation Protocol When a bottleneck occurs, use the 3-Step Escalation: Level 1 (Clinical): Contact the provider's office manager to expedite notes or peer-to-peer reviews. Level 2 (Administrative): Issue a \"Notice of Delay\" to the Adjuster and Plaintiff Attorney, documenting the barrier to care. Level 3 (Legal): Request the Attorney file a \"Motion to Compel\" or use an LOP (Letter of Protection) to bypass insurance stalling."},"51":{"t":"Common Bottlenecks & Challenges","s":"Case Phases - Treatment","w":"Case Phases - Treatment Common Bottlenecks & Challenges Resolve (The Escalation Ladder) When the \"Standard Treatment Map\" hits one of these walls, you move up the ladder: Step A (The Clinician): Request a \"Peer-to-Peer\" review between the treating doctor and the insurance medical director. Step B (The Adjuster): Send a \"Status Update\" explicitly stating that the delay is increasing the total claim cost by extending the period of temporary disability. Step C (The Attorney): If Step B fails, notify the attorney to issue a \"30-Day Demand\" or switch the provider to an LOP (Letter of Protection) basis to bypass the insurance authorization wait times."},"52":{"t":"John Doe vs. Apex Delivery Services (Aggressive Casualty)","s":"Skill Building: Case Phase: Treatment","w":"Skill Building: Case Phase: Treatment Based on the intake, we have a critical complication: The \"Eggshell Plaintiff\" Narrative vs. a prior 2018 Lumbar Strain. \"Eggshell Plaintiff\" Narrative vs. a prior 2018 Lumbar Strain. Based on the intake, we have a critical complication: The \"Eggshell Plaintiff\" Narrative vs. a prior 2018 Lumbar Strain. John Doe vs. Apex Delivery Services (Aggressive Casualty) Services (Aggressive Casualty) John Doe vs. Apex Delivery Services (Aggressive Casualty) The Strategic Conflict Aggressive Casualty (the 3P Carrier) h Aggressive Casualty (the 3P Carrier) has seen the 2018 as seen the 2018 record. They are stalling the MRI for the L4-L5 protrusion noted in your internal intake, claiming the injury is a \"pre- noted in your internal intake, claiming the injury is a \"pre- noted in your internal intake, claiming the injury is a \"pre- existing degenerative condition\" and not caused by the T- existing degenerative condition\" and not caused by the T- bone collision with the Apex truck. bone collision with the Apex truck. The Case Management Objective: You must prove Aggravation. Under US law, an \"Eggshell Plaintiff\" is entitled to full recovery for the increase in disability, even if they were to full recovery for the increase in disability, even if they were to full recovery for the increase in disability, even if they were more vulnerable to injury than a healthy person. more vulnerable to injury than a healthy person. The Strategic Conflict Aggressive Casualty (the 3P Carrier) has seen the 2018 record. They are stalling the MRI for the L4-L5 protrusio"},"53":{"t":"Skill Building: Handling Client Treatment","s":"Skill Building: Handling Client Treatment","w":"Skill Building: Handling Client Treatment Let’s run a \"Flash-Simulation.\" Given the specific client scenario, Your job is to Let’s run a \"Flash-Simulation.\" Given the specific client scenario, Your job is to decide how to handle a common \"Treatment Phase\" obstacle. Create a case file decide how to handle a common \"Treatment Phase\" obstacle. Create a case file in the training interface and update the case file. in the training interface and update the case file. Let’s run a \"Flash-Simulation.\" Given the specific client scenario, Your job is to decide how to handle a common \"Treatment Phase\" obstacle. Create a case file in the training interface and update the case file. 🛑 The Scenario: \"The Transportation Wall\" The Scenario: \"The Transportation Wall\" 🛑 The Scenario: \"The Transportation Wall\" The Plan: The Plan: He is currently in a 12-week intensive Chiro program. He has been doing great for 4 weeks. has been doing great for 4 weeks. The Crisis: John calls you. His car’s transmission just died. He says, \"I can't get to the clinic anymore. It’s a 45- died. He says, \"I can't get to the clinic anymore. It’s a 45- died. He says, \"I can't get to the clinic anymore. It’s a 45- minute drive. I might as well just quit the program and try minute drive. I might as well just quit the program and try minute drive. I might as well just quit the program and try again next year when I have money saved.\" again next year when I have money saved.\" The Plan: He is currently in a 12-week intensive Chiro program. He has been doing great for 4 weeks. The Crisis: John calls you. His car’s transmis"},"54":{"t":"Thank You!","s":"Thank You!","w":"Thank You!"}},"open":[1,2],"close":[54],"topics":[{"h":"What Case Management Is: the General Process & Key Duties","section":"Case Management Fundamentals","pages":[3,4,5]},{"h":"Understanding Case Phases","section":"Case Management Fundamentals","pages":[6]},{"h":"The Intake Phase","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[7,8]},{"h":"Intake and Initial Client Contact","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[9]},{"h":"Case Acceptance Determination","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[10]},{"h":"Common Intake Bottlenecks","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[11,12,13,14,15,16]},{"h":"Tools for Case Planning: the PI Case Lifecycle","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[17]},{"h":"Intake Bottlenecks: Communication Gaps","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[18]},{"h":"Skill Building: the Intake Decision Challenge","section":"Case Phase: Intake","pages":[19,20]},{"h":"Coverage Determination and Liability","section":"Case Phase: Claim Set Up","pages":[21,22,23,24]},{"h":"Case Planning Framework","section":"Case Phase: Claim Set Up","pages":[25,26,27,28]},{"h":"The Treatment Phase","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[29,30,31,32,33]},{"h":"Treatment Protocols and Red Flags","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[34,35]},{"h":"Case Planning: Assessment, SMART Goals and Care Coordination","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[36,37,38]},{"h":"The Standard Treatment Map","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[39,40,41,42,43]},{"h":"Red Flags: the A-C-T Protocol and Course Correction","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[44,45,46,47]},{"h":"Treatment Gaps","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[48,49]},{"h":"Common Treatment Bottlenecks and Challenges","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[50,51]},{"h":"Skill Building: John Doe v. Apex Delivery Services","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[52]},{"h":"Skill Building: Handling Client Treatment","section":"Case Phase: Treatment","pages":[53]}],"w":1920,"h":1080},"2":{"pages":{"1":{"t":"Day 2","s":"Case Management","w":"Case Management Training Day 2"},"2":{"t":"Case Phase : Pre - Demand Case Phase: BI Demand","s":"Training Agenda","w":"Case Phase : Pre - Demand Case Phase: BI Settlement 02 Training Agenda 01 02 03"},"3":{"t":"Core Objectives","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"Valuation Risk Assessment Compliance Pre-demand Case Auditing (often called a \"Forensic Case Audit\" or \"Pre-Litigation Audit\") is the rigorous process of vetting a legal claim’s evidence and value before sending a formal demand letter. Pre- Demand Case Auditing The audit serves as a \"sanity check\" to ensure the claim is bulletproof. Verification Do the facts in the demand letter match the physical evidence (e.g., do the photos of the car crash match the police report)? Are damages calculated accurately by auditing medical bills, lost wages, and future care costs to ensure all expenses are justified? Identifying \"weak spots\" requires an audit that reveals areas where the defense may argue \"comparative negligence,\" implying partial fault on your part. Ensuring the case meets statutory requirements (like the Statute of Limitations) before the demand is issued. Core Objectives"},"4":{"t":"The Auditing Checklist","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"A Full Pre-Demand Audit is the final \"stress test\" of a legal file. It is the transition from collecting information to “weaponizing it.” Pre- Demand Case Auditing The goal of this audit is to ensure that when the demand letter lands on an adjuster's desk, there are zero \"easy outs\" for the defense. The Auditing Checklist Audit Question: Audit Question: A successful audit ensures these three pillars are perfectly synchronized. If one is out of alignment, the case value collapses. The Narrative: The client’s story (how the incident happened). The Physicality: The Property Damage (PD) and biomechanics (the force of impact). The Medicals: The clinical proof of injury. The Trinity of Case Alignment Does the property damage photos support the mechanism of injury described in the medical records?"},"5":{"t":"Master Audit Checklist","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"This is the \"math\" portion of the audit. Mistakes here lead to \"short-funded\" settlements where the client takes home $0. Pre- Demand Case Auditing Master Audit Checklist Sum of all medical bills + Property Damage + Lost Wages. Special Damages Financial & Lien Verification Ensure you have a signed letter from the employer and a \"disability slip\" from the doctor. Without both, adjusters usually reject lost wage claims. Lost Wage Verification Identify all \"Subrogation\" interests. Lien Resolution Statutory: Medicare, Medicaid, ERISA, VA. Consensual: Medical provider liens (LOPs)."},"6":{"t":"Master Audit Checklist","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"A Full Pre-Demand Audit is the final \"stress test\" of a legal file. It is the transition from collecting information to “weaponizing it.” Pre- Demand Case Auditing Master Audit Checklist Verify the final version (not just the exchange of info). Check for citations or \"contributory negligence\" notes. Police/Incident Report Liability & Liability Proof Confirm contact info is still valid. Audit for \"statement drift\" (did their story change?). Witness Statements Review Google Earth or dashcam footage to ensure the physics of the claim are possible. The \"Scene\" Audit"},"7":{"t":"Master Audit Checklist","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"A Full Pre-Demand Audit is the final \"stress test\" of a legal file. It is the transition from collecting information to “weaponizing it.” Pre- Demand Case Auditing Master Audit Checklist Audit for \"point of impact\" clarity. Are there photos of the undercarriage or frame? (Crucial for high-value injury claims). Photos Property Damage (PD) Deep Dive Compare the insurance estimate with the final body shop bill. Look for \"supplementals\" that prove hidden structural damage. Estimates vs. Actuals If totaled, verify the CCC One or valuation report. Ensure the client isn't being lowballed on the asset value. Total Loss/Value"},"8":{"t":"Master Audit Checklist","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"A Full Pre-Demand Audit is the final \"stress test\" of a legal file. It is the transition from collecting information to “weaponizing it.” Pre- Demand Case Auditing Master Audit Checklist Create a timeline. Audit for Gaps in Treatment. If there is a 14-day gap, you must have a documented reason (e.g., \"Client was waiting for MRI authorization\"). Chronology Medical & Billing Audit Scan bills for \"Unbundling\" or duplicate CPT codes that might make the bill look inflated/fraudulent to an adjuster. Coding Audit Review the client's medical history from the past 5–10 years to identify any pre-existing conditions before the defense does. Prior History"},"9":{"t":"The \"Defense-Eye\" Audit (Red Flag Detection)","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"Before finalizing the demand, audit the file for these \"Value Killers\" Pre- Demand Case Auditing The \"Defense-Eye\" Audit (Red Flag Detection) The \"Defense-Eye\" Audit (Red Flag Detection) If they claim \"loss of enjoyment of life\" but posted vacation photos during treatment, adjust the demand or warn the client. Social Media Presence If PD is under $1,000–$2,000, classify it as a \"Minor Impact Soft Tissue\" case and provide stronger medical narratives to counter the \"low damage = no injury\" bias. MIST Designation Confirm the location for filing the suit and determine if the jury pool is \"conservative\" or \"liberal,\" as this affects the \"multiplier\" for pain and suffering. Venue Audit"},"10":{"t":"Final Package Readiness","s":"Pre- Demand Case Auditing","w":"Pro Tip: Always audit the Policy Limits one last time. There is nothing worse than sending a $100,000 demand only to realize mid-negotiation that the defendant has a $25,000 \"Step-Down\" policy. Pre- Demand Case Auditing Final Package Readiness 1-page \"cheat sheet\" of the audit findings. Summary Memo An audited file should be organized as follows for the attorney’s final review: All bills, records, and PD photos labeled. Exhibit Index A calculated demand range based on the policy limits and the \"Total Specials.\" The \"Ask\""},"11":{"t":"Pre-Demand Case Audit (John Doe File)","s":"Skill Building: Audit Challenges","w":"Skill Building: Audit Challenges Pre-Demand Case Audit (John Doe File) Pre-Demand Case Audit (John Doe File) Scenario: The insurance adjuster (Aggressive Casualty) sends an email stating: \"This was a low-speed impact; your client's injuries are exaggerated.\" are exaggerated.\" Scenario: The insurance adjuster (Aggressive Casualty) sends an email stating: \"This was a low-speed impact; your client's injuries are exaggerated.\" Your Challenge: Find two specific details on the intake sheet that Your Challenge: Find two specific details on the intake sheet that Your Challenge: Find two specific details on the intake sheet that objectively disprove this \"low-speed\" argument. objectively disprove this \"low-speed\" argument. Your Challenge: Find two specific details on the intake sheet that objectively disprove this \"low-speed\" argument. Scenario: John didn't see a doctor for 14 days after the ER because Scenario: John didn't see a doctor for 14 days after the ER because Scenario: John didn't see a doctor for 14 days after the ER because he was \"non-responsive due to mental distress.\" The adjuster is moving to deny the claim because of this \"Gap in Treatment.\" moving to deny the claim because of this \"Gap in Treatment.\" moving to deny the claim because of this \"Gap in Treatment.\" Scenario: John didn't see a doctor for 14 days after the ER because he was \"non-responsive due to mental distress.\" The adjuster is moving to deny the claim because of this \"Gap in Treatment.\" Your Challenge: How do you reframe this 14-day gap in the demand Your Challenge: How do you reframe this 14-day gap i"},"12":{"t":"Brainstorming","s":"Skill Building: Audit Challenges","w":"Skill Building: Audit Challenges Pre-Demand Case Audit (John Doe File) Pre-Demand Case Audit (John Doe File) You will receive the files for this case. Conduct a Pre-demand You will receive the files for this case. Conduct a Pre-demand You will receive the files for this case. Conduct a Pre-demand Audit and share your findings with the class. Audit and share your findings with the class. You will receive the files for this case. Conduct a Pre-demand Audit and share your findings with the class. Brainstorming"},"13":{"t":"The Legal & Procedural Foundation","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"The Legal & Procedural Foundation Case Phase : Demand Case Managers must understand the \"why\" behind the paperwork. Bodily Injury (BI) Demand workflows integrate legal expertise, medical understanding, and negotiation skills. Case Managers need to efficiently progress cases from intake to disbursement, meeting deadlines and identifying key \"value-drivers.\" Statute of Limitations (SOL) Liability Framework Coverage Analysis"},"14":{"t":"The Policy Limit Mindset","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"A Case Manager must shift from a reactive mindset to a proactive, strategic one. This phase is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage for resource allocation. Case Phase : Demand The Policy Limit Mindset The goal isn't just to send a packet of records; it is to present the case in a way that makes the insurance adjuster afraid of a jury. Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI): Ensure the client has completed treatment or has a clear future care plan. The \"Gap\" Check: Identifying and explaining any gaps in treatment before the adjuster uses them to devalue the case."},"15":{"t":"🛠 Case Manager Strategies 📄 Demand Package Best Practice","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"TREATMENT GAPS IN DEMAND PHASE Case Phase : Demand 🛠 Case Manager Strategies Identify gaps early during record review Insurance adjusters often use treatment gaps to argue that: The injuries were not serious The client recovered earlier than claimed Medical treatment was unnecessary or excessive Other unrelated factors caused the condition 👉 Unexplained gaps can reduce settlement value. Document the reason for every treatment interruption Obtain supporting documents when available Include explanations directly in the demand package timeline When addressing a gap: Acknowledge it clearly Provide context and supporting facts Connect continued symptoms to ongoing treatment Reinforce consistency of complaints before and after the gap 👉 A well-explained treatment gap is less damaging than an unexplained one. 📄 Demand Package Best Practice"},"16":{"t":"The Demand Packet Checklist","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"A Case Manager must shift from a reactive mindset to a proactive, strategic one. This phase is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage for resource allocation. Component CM Responsibility Medical Specials A clean, itemized spreadsheet of every provider, date of service, and total bill. The Narrative Drafting the \"Human Story\"—how did the injury affect their job, parenting, or hobbies? Liability Proof Including the police report, witness statements, and clear photos of property damage. Special Damages Lost wage verifications (W2s or employer letters) and out-of-pocket receipts. Case Phase : Demand The Demand Packet Checklist A Case Manager must ensure the \"Big Four\" components are flawless before the attorney reviews the demand letter:"},"17":{"t":"Quality Control","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"A Case Manager must shift from a reactive mindset to a proactive, strategic one. This phase is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage for resource allocation. Case Phase : Demand Quality Control Before a demand is sent, the CM should perform a \"Final Scrub\" to catch common \"adjuster traps\": Billing Overlaps: Ensure no double-billing for the same date of service. Pre-existing Conditions: Highlight how the accident aggravated a previous injury rather than trying to hide it. Balance Verification: Call every provider to get the \"final balance\" so the demand amount is 100% accurate."},"18":{"t":"Negotiating the \"First Call\" (For Senior CMs)","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"A Case Manager must shift from a reactive mindset to a proactive, strategic one. This phase is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage for resource allocation. Case Phase : Demand Negotiating the \"First Call\" (For Senior CMs) Need to Know: The \"Anchor\" Technique: Never apologize for a high demand. Stay firm on the value drivers. Note-Taking: Document every excuse the adjuster gives (e.g., \"low impact,\" \"delayed treatment\"). This is the ammunition the attorney needs for litigation."},"19":{"t":"Best Practices","s":"Case Phase : Demand","w":"A Case Manager must shift from a reactive mindset to a proactive, strategic one. This phase is all about identifying needs, quantifying requirements, and setting the stage for resource allocation. Case Phase : Demand Best Practices 30-Day Rule: Once a client is MMI, the demand must be out the door within 30 days. Photo Impact: Always lead the demand with the most \"graphic\" photo (property damage or injury) to set the tone. The \"Why\" Factor: Every medical bill added to the demand needs a corresponding \"Why\"—why was this treatment necessary for the client's recovery? CM Pro-Tip: \"A demand is not a request for money; it is a presentation of a debt owed. Treat the insurance company like a bank that hasn't paid its bill.\""},"20":{"t":"Real-Time Demand Audit","s":"Skill Building","w":"Skill Building : Demand Phase Challenge : Demand Phase Challenge Skill Building : Demand Phase Challenge Real-Time Demand Audit: John Doe v. Apex Delivery Demand Packet Your demand specialist has submitted a draft for your review. Your goal is to identify and close any potential loopholes, ensuring the presentation is airtight so the adjuster has no grounds to dispute or decline the claim. If any deficiencies are found, assign tasks accordingly to rectify the documentation and finalize the demand. CMS"},"21":{"t":"THE “PITCH”","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE “PITCH” “PITCH” THE “PITCH” In PI law, the demand letter is your opening move. If it looks like a template, the adjuster will treat it like one. Adjusters typically protect insurance companies by undervaluing claims, so it's important to be prepared for negotiations. Negotiation Battle Map Create a Net Sheet Calculate total medical liens, costs, and attorney fees; Calculate total medical liens, costs, and attorney fees; anything below this total means the client receives $0. anything below this total means the client receives $0. The aim is to maximize the net settlement amount. The aim is to maximize the net settlement amount. Calculate total medical liens, costs, and attorney fees; anything below this total means the client receives $0. The aim is to maximize the net settlement amount. Identify the Value Drivers List 3 facts that would scare a jury. List 3 facts that would scare a jury. Example: \"The defendant was on his phone\" or \"The client missed his daughter’s wedding due to surgery.\" missed his daughter’s wedding due to surgery.\" List 3 facts that would scare a jury. Example: \"The defendant was on his phone\" or \"The client missed his daughter’s wedding due to surgery.\" Anticipate the Adjuster’s Response Identify your top two weaknesses and prepare to justify them, ideally with supporting documentation. them, ideally with supporting documentation. Identify your top two weaknesses and prepare to justify them, ideally with supporting documentation. Prepare the Rebuttal Have the argument ready before they bring it up. Have the argument ready b"},"22":{"t":"THE VALUATION BASELINE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE VALUATION BASELINE In Bodily Injury (BI) settlements, PIP benefits are paid regardless of fault, complicating compensation claims from the at-fault party. Case Managers need to understand the relationship between these coverages to ensure successful settlements and prevent clients from getting ZERO compensation. In most jurisdictions, the defendant’s insurance carrier is entitled to a set-off for payments made by PIP. PIP. In most jurisdictions, the defendant’s insurance carrier is entitled to a set-off for payments made by PIP. The \"Set-Off\" The \"Set-Off\" (The Credit) (The Credit) The \"Set-Off\" (The Credit) The Logic: The law generally prevents \"double recovery.\" If PIP already paid the hospital The law generally prevents \"double recovery.\" If PIP already paid the hospital $10,000, the defendant shouldn't have to pay that same $10,000 again. $10,000, the defendant shouldn't have to pay that same $10,000 again. The Calculation: The Calculation: {Gross Settlement Value} - {PIP Paid Amount} = {Defendant's Remaining Liability} {Gross Settlement Value} - {PIP Paid Amount} = {Defendant's Remaining Liability} The Logic: The law generally prevents \"double recovery.\" If PIP already paid the hospital $10,000, the defendant shouldn't have to pay that same $10,000 again. The Calculation: {Gross Settlement Value} - {PIP Paid Amount} = {Defendant's Remaining Liability}"},"23":{"t":"THE VALUATION BASELINE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE VALUATION BASELINE BI Adjusters use PIP payments to gauge the \"seriousness\" of a claim during the Demand Phase. \"seriousness\" of a claim during the Demand Phase. BI Adjusters use PIP payments to gauge the \"seriousness\" of a claim during the Demand Phase. PIP as a Valuation Baseline S p e d o f E x h a u s t i o n S p e d o f E x h a u s t i o n S p e d o f E x h a u s t i o n If a client exhausts a $10,000 PIP limit in 48 hours (like John Doe did with If a client exhausts a $10,000 PIP limit in 48 hours (like John Doe did with his ER and EMS bills), it signals a high-severity case. his ER and EMS bills), it signals a high-severity case. If a client exhausts a $10,000 PIP limit in 48 hours (like John Doe did with his ER and EMS bills), it signals a high-severity case. S p e c i a l D a m a g e s S p e c i a l D a m a g e s S p e c i a l D a m a g e s The total medical bills (the \"Specials\") are usually much higher than The total medical bills (the \"Specials\") are usually much higher than what PIP pays. Case Managers must argue that even though PIP covered what PIP pays. Case Managers must argue that even though PIP covered $10k, the total value of the bills ($41,400 in John’s case) is the true $10k, the total value of the bills ($41,400 in John’s case) is the true anchor for pain and suffering multipliers. anchor for pain and suffering multipliers. The total medical bills (the \"Specials\") are usually much higher than what PIP pays. Case Managers must argue that even though PIP covered $10k, the total value of the bills ($41,400 in John’s case) is "},"24":{"t":"THE VALUATION BASELINE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE VALUATION BASELINE As seen in John Doe’s file with Dr. Sarah Spine, the As seen in John Doe’s file with Dr. Sarah Spine, the Emergency Medical Condition (EMC) finding is a major negotiation lever. major negotiation lever. As seen in John Doe’s file with Dr. Sarah Spine, the Emergency Medical Condition (EMC) finding is a major negotiation lever. The \"EMC\" and the Benefit Ceiling W i t h o u t a n E M C W i t h o u t a n E M C W i t h o u t a n E M C In many states, PIP is capped at $2,500. This forces more bills onto the BI In many states, PIP is capped at $2,500. This forces more bills onto the BI settlement, often \"eating up\" the client's net recovery. settlement, often \"eating up\" the client's net recovery. In many states, PIP is capped at $2,500. This forces more bills onto the BI settlement, often \"eating up\" the client's net recovery. W i t h a n E M C The full $10,000 is available. This is better for negotiations because it The full $10,000 is available. This is better for negotiations because it lowers the amount of \"unpaid\" medical liens that need to be negotiated lowers the amount of \"unpaid\" medical liens that need to be negotiated down at the end of the case. down at the end of the case. The full $10,000 is available. This is better for negotiations because it lowers the amount of \"unpaid\" medical liens that need to be negotiated down at the end of the case."},"25":{"t":"THE VALUATION BASELINE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE VALUATION BASELINE This is the most critical technical detail in a BI negotiation: negotiation: This is the most critical technical detail in a BI negotiation: Subrogation vs. Subrogation vs. Non- Non- Subrogation vs. Non- Subrogation N o n - S u b r o g a t i o n ( C o m o n ) N o n - S u b r o g a t i o n ( C o m o n ) N o n - S u b r o g a t i o n ( C o m o n ) The PIP carrier cannot ask for their $10,000 back from the BI settlement. The PIP carrier cannot ask for their $10,000 back from the BI settlement. This is a win for the client, as that money stays in their pocket (via paid This is a win for the client, as that money stays in their pocket (via paid bills). bills). The PIP carrier cannot ask for their $10,000 back from the BI settlement. This is a win for the client, as that money stays in their pocket (via paid bills). S u b r o g a t i o n S u b r o g a t i o n S u b r o g a t i o n The PIP carrier places a lien on the BI settlement. The Case Manager must The PIP carrier places a lien on the BI settlement. The Case Manager must then negotiate with the PIP adjuster to reduce that lien so the client then negotiate with the PIP adjuster to reduce that lien so the client keeps more of the final check. keeps more of the final check. The PIP carrier places a lien on the BI settlement. The Case Manager must then negotiate with the PIP adjuster to reduce that lien so the client keeps more of the final check."},"26":{"t":"THE VALUATION BASELINE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE VALUATION BASELINE A savvy Case Manager often wants PIP exhausted as quickly as possible on the \"hard costs\" (ER, as quickly as possible on the \"hard costs\" (ER, Imaging). Imaging). A savvy Case Manager often wants PIP exhausted as quickly as possible on the \"hard costs\" (ER, Imaging). Strategic \"Exhaustion\"Exhaustion\" Strategic \"Exhaustion\" W h y ? W h y ? Once PIP is exhausted, you can move the client to Letters of Protection Once PIP is exhausted, you can move the client to Letters of Protection (LOP) for subsequent treatment (Chiro, PT). (LOP) for subsequent treatment (Chiro, PT). Once PIP is exhausted, you can move the client to Letters of Protection (LOP) for subsequent treatment (Chiro, PT). N e g o t i a t i o n L e v e r a g e N e g o t i a t i o n L e v e r a g e N e g o t i a t i o n L e v e r a g e LOP providers are often more willing to reduce their bills at the end of a LOP providers are often more willing to reduce their bills at the end of a case than a hospital is. case than a hospital is. By \"spending\" the PIP money on the non-negotiable hospital bills, you By \"spending\" the PIP money on the non-negotiable hospital bills, you preserve the BI settlement funds for the client. preserve the BI settlement funds for the client. LOP providers are often more willing to reduce their bills at the end of a case than a hospital is. By \"spending\" the PIP money on the non-negotiable hospital bills, you preserve the BI settlement funds for the client. Tip: Always verify the PIP Ledger before beginning BI negotiations. If you tell an adjuster t"},"27":{"t":"NEGOTIATIONS INITIATED","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations NEGOTIATIONS INITIATED The negotiations officially begin after sending the Demand Packet to the Adjuster. The “Battle” The \"Soft Lead.\" The \"Soft Lead.\" First Call Confirm they received the demand and all records. \"Hi, I'm calling regarding the John Doe demand. I want to make sure calling regarding the John Doe demand. I want to make sure you saw the MRI report on page 14 specifically.\" you saw the MRI report on page 14 specifically.\" Confirm they received the demand and all records. \"Hi, I'm calling regarding the John Doe demand. I want to make sure you saw the MRI report on page 14 specifically.\" Set the Tone State that the file is \"Litigation Ready.\" State that the file is \"Litigation Ready.\" The Ask If they haven't made an offer, ask: \"Based on the clear If they haven't made an offer, ask: \"Based on the clear liability and the $40k in specials, what is your opening evaluation?\" evaluation?\" If they haven't made an offer, ask: \"Based on the clear liability and the $40k in specials, what is your opening evaluation?\" The Response to the Low-Ball Never get angry. Use the \"Professional Pause.\" Count to five after they give a low number. Then say: \"I’m struggling five after they give a low number. Then say: \"I’m struggling to see how that number accounts for the permanent nature of the injury. What data are you using to get there?\" of the injury. What data are you using to get there?\" Never get angry. Use the \"Professional Pause.\" Count to five after they give a low number. Then say: \"I’m struggling to see how that number accounts for the permanent nat"},"28":{"t":"SETTLEMENT NEGOTIATIONS","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations SETTLEMENT NEGOTIATIONS When you are at $100k and they are at $30k, the gap feels impossible. Bracketing builds a bridge. The Bracketing Technique Propose the Logic \"We are $70k apart. If I can move my Attorney into the $80k \"We are $70k apart. If I can move my Attorney into the $80k range, can you get into the $50k range?\" range, can you get into the $50k range?\" \"We are $70k apart. If I can move my Attorney into the $80k range, can you get into the $50k range?\" Check Authority If they say \"I don't have that much,\" ask: \"Who does? If they say \"I don't have that much,\" ask: \"Who does? Because if we can't get into the $50s, we are wasting Because if we can't get into the $50s, we are wasting each other's time and should just file the complaint.\" each other's time and should just file the complaint.\" If they say \"I don't have that much,\" ask: \"Who does? Because if we can't get into the $50s, we are wasting each other's time and should just file the complaint.\" Close the Bracket Once you are both in the $50k–$80k range, you negotiate the \"middle\" (e.g., $65k). the \"middle\" (e.g., $65k). Once you are both in the $50k–$80k range, you negotiate the \"middle\" (e.g., $65k)."},"29":{"t":"COMMON ADJUSTER \"STALL TACTICS\"","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations COMMON ADJUSTER \"STALL TACTICS\"STALL TACTICS\" COMMON ADJUSTER \"STALL TACTICS\" Adjusters use \"stalls\" to wear you down so you accept a lower end-of-month settlement. The Stall \"I'm waiting on \"I'm waiting on my manager's authority.\" authority.\" \"I'm waiting on my manager's authority.\" \"Understood. I'll give you until Thursday at 4:00 PM. If I \"Understood. I'll give you until Thursday at 4:00 PM. If I don't hear back, I've been instructed to send the file to don't hear back, I've been instructed to send the file to our litigation department.\" our litigation department.\" \"Understood. I'll give you until Thursday at 4:00 PM. If I don't hear back, I've been instructed to send the file to our litigation department.\" \"Those aren't relevant to this acute injury. However, \"Those aren't relevant to this acute injury. However, if you want them, I expect an 'In-Good-Faith' offer on the current undisputed injuries today.\" the current undisputed injuries today.\" \"Those aren't relevant to this acute injury. However, if you want them, I expect an 'In-Good-Faith' offer on the current undisputed injuries today.\" \"The property \"The property damage was too low for this injury.\" injury.\" \"The property damage was too low for this injury.\" \"Physics doesn't work that way. Low-velocity impacts \"Physics doesn't work that way. Low-velocity impacts often cause high-torque injuries to the spine. We have the MRI to prove it.\" the MRI to prove it.\" \"Physics doesn't work that way. Low-velocity impacts often cause high-torque injuries to the spine. We have the MRI to prove it.\" The "},"30":{"t":"CLOSE THE DEAL","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations CLOSE THE DEAL The last $2,500 is often the hardest to get. The \"Final-Final\" The \"Split the Baby\" Move We are at $47k, you're at $43k. Let's meet at $45k, and We are at $47k, you're at $43k. Let's meet at $45k, and I'll get the release signed within the hour.\" I'll get the release signed within the hour.\" We are at $47k, you're at $43k. Let's meet at $45k, and I'll get the release signed within the hour.\" Before hanging up, verify: Before hanging up, verify: Is this for \"Bodily Injury\" only? (Save Property Damage for later if not settled). for later if not settled). Does this include all liens? Does this include all liens? When will the check be mailed? When will the check be mailed? Before hanging up, verify: Is this for \"Bodily Injury\" only? (Save Property Damage for later if not settled). Does this include all liens? When will the check be mailed? The \"Paper Trail\" The \"Paper Trail\" Immediately email the adjuster: \"Per our conversation, Immediately email the adjuster: \"Per our conversation, we have settled the John Doe matter for $45,000. Please send the release.\" send the release.\" Immediately email the adjuster: \"Per our conversation, we have settled the John Doe matter for $45,000. Please send the release.\" Confirm the Terms"},"31":{"t":"THE “NEVER” RULE THE “ NEVER ” RULE","s":"Settlement Negotiations","w":"Settlement Negotiations THE “NEVER” RULE Never apologize for a high demand. Never apologize for a high demand. It’s based on the client’s suffering. It’s based on the client’s suffering. Never apologize for a high demand. It’s based on the client’s suffering. Never bid against yourself. If you drop your price, the adjuster must raise theirs before you move again. adjuster must raise theirs before you move again. Never bid against yourself. If you drop your price, the adjuster must raise theirs before you move again. Never accept \"That's all I have\" as the final answer on the first day. Every adjuster has a supervisor with a bigger checkbook. a bigger checkbook. Never accept \"That's all I have\" as the final answer on the first day. Every adjuster has a supervisor with a bigger checkbook."},"32":{"t":"YOUR SLUM-DUNK LIABILITY CASE THE","s":"Skill Building : Settlement Negotiations","w":"Tasks Skill Building : Settlement Negotiations THE MATH CHECK The clock is ticking on the John Doe file. Liability is a total Slam Dunk because the Apex commercial delivery truck ran a red light. However, the defense adjuster is trying to starve your client out. They have targeted John’s 2018 back strain and his 14-day treatment gap to throw out a lowball, \"take-it-or- leave-it\" pre-suit offer. Refer to the Email Exchange from you and the adjuster. TASK 1: The Integrity Audit (Accept or Reject?) TASK 1: The Integrity Audit (Accept or Reject?) Dive back into the John Doe Demand Packet and the Mock Email Exchange. Dive back into the John Doe Demand Packet and the Mock Email Exchange. Audit the real medical and legal ledger. Audit the real medical and legal ledger. Your Goal: Run the net math against the $55,000.00 offer. Is this offer legally Your Goal: Run the net math against the $55,000.00 offer. Is this offer legally acceptable, or does it leave your client completely in the red? Prove your acceptable, or does it leave your client completely in the red? Prove your conclusion with hard numbers. conclusion with hard numbers. TASK 1: The Integrity Audit (Accept or Reject?) Dive back into the John Doe Demand Packet and the Mock Email Exchange. Audit the real medical and legal ledger. Your Goal: Run the net math against the $55,000.00 offer. Is this offer legally acceptable, or does it leave your client completely in the red? Prove your conclusion with hard numbers. YOUR SLUM-DUNK LIABILITY CASE Tasks Refer to the Demand Packet ⚔ TASK 2: Break the Adjuster (The Rebuttal) If th"},"33":{"t":"Phase II: The UM/UIM \"Safety Check\"","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"If the at-fault driver has low or no insurance, you must tap into the client’s own Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist (UM/UIM) coverage. Phase II: The UM/UIM \"Safety Check\" The Subrogation Waiver (The \"Green Light\") Relevance: If you settle with the bad driver and sign their release without getting a waiver from your client's own insurance, you may destroy your client's right to collect UM/UIM benefits. The \"30-Day Letter\" Send a formal notice to the UM carrier notifying them of the offer. The Waiver Document Secure a written Waiver of Subrogation from the UM carrier. This proves they have given up their right to sue the driver, allowing you to settle the liability claim and proceed with the UM claim. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"34":{"t":"The Legal & Strategic Foundation","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"Before a settlement can be discussed, the \"file\" must be bulletproof. The Legal & Strategic Foundation Key Laws & Doctrines The Made Whole Doctrine This tool states that a health insurer (subrogated carrier) cannot seek claims from a settlement unless the client is \"fully compensated.\" This principle can help persuade lienholders to waive their claims if the settlement is minor relative to significant injuries. The Common Fund Doctrine Requires lienholders to reduce their bill by the Attorney’s fee percentage (usually $33.3\\%$). Logic: They shouldn't get a \"free ride\" on the work the law firm did to recover the money. Statute of Limitations (SOL) The \"death date\" for a case. CMs must track this religiously; missing it is an automatic malpractice event. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"35":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"This is the most critical document in the file. It is the contract that legally ends the dispute. The Execution The Release of All Claims (The \"Exit\" Document) Release of Bodily Injury (BI) Ensure the client isn't accidentally signing away their Property Damage (PD) or Personal Injury Protection (PIP) claims if those are still being negotiated separately. Non-Admission of Liability Standard releases state that the insurance company is paying to avoid further litigation, not because they admit their driver was at fault. Indemnity & Hold Harmless Clause This clause states that the claimant (and their CM/Attorney) is responsible for paying all medical liens. If a hospital sues the insurance company later for an unpaid bill, the claimant must \"indemnify\" (pay back) the insurer for those costs. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"36":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"Often required by state law or internal compliance, this document ensures the claimant understands exactly what is happening. The Execution Settlement Disclosure Statement (The \"Truth\" Document) \"Full and Final\" It explicitly states that even if the claimant's back starts hurting again next year, they cannot come back for more money. Cooling-off Period Some states have a \"rescission period\" (e.g., 2–3 business days) where a claimant can change their mind after signing. The CM must track this date before cutting checks. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"37":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"A CM cannot rely on a medical bill alone; they need a formal \"Final Demand\" or \"Payoff Letter.\" The Execution Lien Payoff Letters (The \"Verification\" Paperwork) Medicare Final Demand If the claimant is 65+ or on SSDI, the CMS (Center for Medicare & Medicaid Services) will issue a final demand. Warning: This document can take 60+ days to arrive; the CM must keep the file \"pended\" until it is in hand. Satisfaction of Lien Once a provider (like a chiropractor or surgeon) is paid, the CM must obtain a signed letter stating the lien is \"Satisfied.\" This prevents the provider from sending the client to collections later. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"38":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"This is the ledger that breaks down the \"Gross to Net.\" As a CM, you are auditing this for accuracy. The Execution The Settlement Statement (The \"Net\" Sheet) Cost Verification Ensure \"Case Costs\" (e.g., $50 for police reports, $200 for medical records) are backed by receipts. The \"Net to Client\" This is the final number the client actually receives. The CM’s goal is to ensure this number is sufficient to cover any future medical needs discussed during the treatment phase. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"39":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"If the case was already in litigation (a lawsuit was filed), the settlement is not over until the court is notified. The Execution The Dismissal (The \"Court\" Filing) Dismissal with Prejudice This means the case is closed and cannot be refiled. Dismissal without Prejudice Rare in settlements, this allows the case to be refiled under specific conditions. The CM should verify that \"With Prejudice\" is the language used to ensure total file closure. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"40":{"t":"The Execution","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"Document Who Signs? Purpose Release Claimant Ends the right to sue. Final Demand Lienholder (Medicare/Hospital) States the exact amount needed to clear debt. W-9 Payee (Law Firm/Claimant) Tax reporting for the IRS. Stipulation to Dismiss Attorneys Notifies the court the case is over. Many insurance carriers will not issue a settlement check until they have a W-9 Form from the law firm or the claimant. If the CM doesn't request this the moment the verbal agreement is made, the check will be delayed by at least a week. The Execution W-9 Comparison of Key Post-Settlement Documents Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"41":{"t":"Finalized Settlement","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"Once the settlement is finalized and the paperwork is understood, the Case Manager (CM) enters the \"Technical Closing\" stage. Your primary mission here is to move the file from \"Settled\" to \"Paid & Archived\" while ensuring total compliance. Finalized Settlement Boots-on-the-ground Tasks Administrative Document Control The Signature Chase: Send the Release of All Claims to the claimant via secure e-signature (like DocuSign) or schedule an in-person signing. Notary Coordination: If the release requires a notary (common for high-value BI claims), verify the notary’s stamp is current and the ID matches the claimant exactly. W-9 Procurement: Immediately collect a W-9 from the claimant or the firm to prevent the insurance carrier’s accounting department from \"flagging\" the payment. Case Phase: BI Settlement"},"42":{"t":"Finalized Settlement","s":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","w":"Once the settlement is finalized and the paperwork is understood, the Case Manager (CM) enters the \"Technical Closing\" stage. Your primary mission here is to move the file from \"Settled\" to \"Paid & Archived\" while ensuring total compliance. Case Phase: BI Settlement Finalized Settlement Boots-on-the-Ground Tasks Final Lien Mitigation (The \"Reduction\" ) Provider Negotiation: Call the billing departments of all lienholders. The Script: \"The settlement was lower than expected. In the interest of global resolution, will you accept 50% of the balance as payment in full?\" Update the Ledger: Immediately reflect any \"savings\" or reductions in the Settlement Statement to show the client their net recovery is increasing. Medicare/Medicaid Finalization: Log into the MSPRP (Medicare Secondary Payer Recovery Portal) to ensure the \"Final Demand\" matches your internal ledger."},"43":{"t":"Group Discussion : Caught in an Operational Pincer Movement","s":"Skill Building : BI Settlement","w":"Skill Building : BI Settlement Group Discussion : Caught in an Operational Pincer Movement Liability for John Doe is clear as Apex Delivery Services' vehicle ran a red light, T-boning John's vehicle and causing significant structural damage that required mechanical extrication. Aggressive Casualty Insurance has been pressuring you, targeting John's 2018 workplace lumbar strain and a 14-day treatment gap to justify a low, \"take-it-or-leave-it\" offer. Adjuster’s Pressure: 'I need the signed release emailed to me in 5 minutes, or I am revoking the $100,000 policy limits tender permanently.' The clock is ticking.” The team established a conservative settlement target for basic medical specials. Prior to client signing, issues emerge. Client is Furious. Dr. Spine reports permanent nerve damage for John Doe. A 5% Whole Person Impairment rating indicates an irreversible neurological deficit. What is your exact step-by-step action plan right now to save the file, save the client, and save the firm from a malpractice suit? The specific verbal script for de-escalating John Doe regarding a bankrupt defendant The action plan on how to mitigate a pending malpractice lawsuit The precise statutory measure to counter the adjuster's 5-minute revocation threat You must provide:"},"44":{"t":"Thank You!","s":"Thank You!","w":"Thank You!"}},"open":[1,2],"close":[44],"topics":[{"h":"Pre-Demand Auditing: Core Objectives and the Auditing Checklist","section":"Pre-Demand Case Auditing","pages":[3,4]},{"h":"The Master Audit Checklist","section":"Pre-Demand Case Auditing","pages":[5,6,7,8]},{"h":"The \"Defense-Eye\" Audit: Red Flag Detection","section":"Pre-Demand Case Auditing","pages":[9]},{"h":"Final Package Readiness","section":"Pre-Demand Case Auditing","pages":[10]},{"h":"Skill Building: Pre-Demand Case Audit","section":"Pre-Demand Case Auditing","pages":[11,12]},{"h":"The Demand: Legal and Procedural Foundation","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[13]},{"h":"The Policy Limit Mindset","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[14]},{"h":"Case Manager Strategies and Demand Package Best Practice","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[15]},{"h":"The Demand Packet Checklist and Quality Control","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[16,17]},{"h":"Negotiating the \"First Call\" and Best Practices","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[18,19]},{"h":"Skill Building: Real-Time Demand Audit","section":"Case Phase: Demand","pages":[20]},{"h":"Settlement Negotiations: the \"Pitch\"","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[21]},{"h":"The Valuation Baseline","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[22,23,24,25,26]},{"h":"Negotiations Initiated","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[27,28]},{"h":"Common Adjuster \"Stall Tactics\"","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[29]},{"h":"Closing the Deal and the \"Never\" Rule","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[30,31]},{"h":"Skill Building: Settlement Negotiations","section":"Settlement Negotiations","pages":[32]},{"h":"BI Settlement: the UM/UIM \"Safety Check\" and Foundation","section":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","pages":[33,34]},{"h":"BI Settlement: the Execution","section":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","pages":[35,36,37,38,39,40]},{"h":"Finalized Settlement","section":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","pages":[41,42]},{"h":"Skill Building: BI Settlement","section":"Case Phase: BI Settlement","pages":[43]}],"w":1920,"h":1080},"3":{"pages":{"1":{"t":"Day 3","s":"Case Management","w":"Case Management Training Day 3"},"2":{"t":"Case Phase- UM Case Phase- UM Settlement","s":"Training Agenda","w":"Case Phase- UM Settlement Lien Negotiations, Reduction Phase 02 Training Agenda 01 02 03"},"3":{"t":"Why this Phase Matters","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"The Reality: Case Phases : UM Demand Why this Phase Matters Many junior case managers think their job is done when a high settlement number is Many junior case managers think their job is done when a high settlement number is reached. You must instill the mindset that the gross settlement amount doesn’t matter to reached. You must instill the mindset that the gross settlement amount doesn’t matter to the client; only the net check matters. the client; only the net check matters. Many junior case managers think their job is done when a high settlement number is reached. You must instill the mindset that the gross settlement amount doesn’t matter to the client; only the net check matters. A $50,000 settlement with $45,000 in un-reduced medical liens means the client walks away with practically nothing after attorney fees. The Goal: The case manager's efficiency in this phase directly dictates whether a client leaves happy or files a bar complaint."},"4":{"t":"Beyond Organization: The UM Demand Strategy","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"The Mindset Shift Case Phases : UM Demand Beyond Organization: The UM Demand Strategy The UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Demand Phase is crucial for clients seeking The UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Demand Phase is crucial for clients seeking funds after accidents with underinsured drivers, serving as a financial safety net. funds after accidents with underinsured drivers, serving as a financial safety net. However, insurance companies frequently resist full payouts, and failure in this phase However, insurance companies frequently resist full payouts, and failure in this phase can result in financial loss for clients and legal malpractice risks for law firms. can result in financial loss for clients and legal malpractice risks for law firms. The UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Demand Phase is crucial for clients seeking funds after accidents with underinsured drivers, serving as a financial safety net. However, insurance companies frequently resist full payouts, and failure in this phase can result in financial loss for clients and legal malpractice risks for law firms. The Old Mindset: \"My job is to gather medical records, staple them to a demand letter, and mail them to the adjuster.\" The Goal The Master Mindset: In the UM phase, you are no longer just organizing paperwork. You are building an undeniable legal trap. The Bottom Line: If you build this trap correctly, the insurance company is backed into a corner and has no choice but to pay the policy limits."},"5":{"t":"BI Exhaustion & Consent","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"Mechanic 1 – Locking the Escape Hatches Case Phases : UM Demand BI Exhaustion & Consent When the at-fault party's insurance pays out their full policy limit (settles), but your damages exceed that amount, you \"exhaust\" their policy and pivot to your own carrier to make up the difference. carrier to make up the difference. When the at-fault party's insurance pays out their full policy limit (settles), but your damages exceed that amount, you \"exhaust\" their policy and pivot to your own carrier to make up the difference. Filing a UM/UIM Demand occurs after settling the primary Bodily Injury (BI) settling the primary Bodily Injury (BI) claim, marking a distinct second phase in a Personal Injury case. a Personal Injury case. Filing a UM/UIM Demand occurs after settling the primary Bodily Injury (BI) claim, marking a distinct second phase in a Personal Injury case. Insurance adjusters love technicalities. Your job is to kill those excuses on page one. Front-Load the Proof: Place the third-party declaration sheet, the signed liability release, and the UIM carrier’s written Consent to Settle form at the absolute front of your packet. The Result: You strip the adjuster of their favorite defense: claiming the client breached the contract. You prove the contract trigger has been pulled."},"6":{"t":"The UM Demand Package","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"Turning Risk Management Against the Insurer Case Phases : UM Demand The UM Demand Package Once the BI Case is closed, You will assign a task to your Once the BI Case is closed, You will assign a task to your Demand Specialist to create a new demand for the client’s own insurance company. own insurance company. Once the BI Case is closed, You will assign a task to your Demand Specialist to create a new demand for the client’s own insurance company. You will then perform a comprehensive audit similar to the one conducted in BI Demand. Demand. You will then perform a comprehensive audit similar to the one conducted in BI Demand. When hard medical debt drastically exceeds the available policy limits, the adjuster faces a dangerous business dilemma. The Ultimate Leverage: They must choose between paying a capped policy limit today, or risking uncapped financial exposure in front of a jury tomorrow. The Bad-Faith Trap If they refuse to pay the policy limits in the face of undisputed, overwhelming damages, they expose their own company to a first-party bad-faith lawsuit."},"7":{"t":"Investigation & Evaluation","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"The Weak File The Legal Trap Sending 300 pages of raw, messy chiropractic charts. Sending a 2-page summary matching clear diagnostic codes to specific treatments. A casual cover letter stating: \"Our client hurts, please pay the limits.\" A formal package detailing a $40,000 surgical recommendation alongside a strict 30-day clock. Result: A $3,500 lowball offer because the file carries no legal risk. Result: A full policy-limits payout because the insurer's legal risk is too high. Case Phases : UM Demand Investigation & Evaluation Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\" Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\" Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\""},"8":{"t":"The Demand Packet","s":"Case Phases : UM Demand","w":"The Document Inclusions Case Phases : UM Demand The Demand Packet To secure a UM Settlement, your demand package must show the other driver’s fault and that the damages exceed To secure a UM Settlement, your demand package must show the other driver’s fault and that the damages exceed what you've collected. what you've collected. To secure a UM Settlement, your demand package must show the other driver’s fault and that the damages exceed what you've collected. Evidence of Liability Police/Crash Report Photos of Property Damage Witness Statements Dashcam/Surveillance Footage(If available) Economic Damages Itemized Medical Bills Complete Medical Records Lost Wage Verification Future Medical Estimates Non-Economic Damages The Demand Letter Impact Statement Photos of Injuries Proof of Exhaustion The Settlement Release The Check Copy Declaration Page of At-Fault Driver Consent to Settle Letter"},"9":{"t":"The UM Settlement","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"Component Example Calculation Total Case Value $100,000 (Medical bills + Pain & Suffering) BI Settlement Received – $25,000 (At-fault driver's max limit) UM Settlement Goal $75,000 (Remaining balance) How the Settlement is Calculated Case Phases : UM Settlement The UM Settlement A UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Settlement is the second half of your client’s recovery process. the second half of your client’s recovery process. A UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Settlement is the second half of your client’s recovery process. Because you are now negotiating with your client’s own insurance company, the dynamic shifts from a third-party claim to a first-party contract claim. a first-party contract claim. Because you are now negotiating with your client’s own insurance company, the dynamic shifts from a third-party claim to a first-party contract claim. The UM insurer doesn't just pay your client’s policy limit; they pay the \"gap\" between the total damages and what was already collected."},"10":{"t":"Negotiation & Settlement","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"The Offer Negotiation & Settlement Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\" Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\" Even though they are your Client’s own insurance company, they now become \"adversarial.\" The adjuster will make an initial offer. It is rarely the full amount of the demand. Back-and-Forth CMs will negotiate to move the claim closer to the actual value of the remaining damages. Resolution Most UM claims end here with a supplemental settlement check. Case Phases : UM Settlement"},"11":{"t":"The UM Settlement","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"The \"Offset\" Rule The UM Settlement A UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Settlement is the second half of your client’s recovery process. the second half of your client’s recovery process. A UM (Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist) Settlement is the second half of your client’s recovery process. Because you are now negotiating with your client’s own insurance company, the dynamic shifts from a third-party claim to a first-party contract claim. a first-party contract claim. Because you are now negotiating with your client’s own insurance company, the dynamic shifts from a third-party claim to a first-party contract claim. In many states, the Client’s insurer is allowed to \"offset\" or subtract the BI settlement from their UM limits. If you have a $50,000 UM policy and already got $25,000 from the BI, they may only have $25,000 in \"room\" left to collect, unless they have \"Add-ons\" or \"Stacked\" coverage. The Timeline Once the BI case settles, the UM portion can proceed quickly since evidence like medical records and police reports is already collected. Demand Phase Evaluation Negotiation Total Time Case Phases : UM Settlement"},"12":{"t":"Challenges","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"The \"Value\" Dispute Challenges Even though the client are their customer, the insurance company will look for reasons to pay less: Even though the client are their customer, the insurance company will look for reasons to pay less: Even though the client are their customer, the insurance company will look for reasons to pay less: They may agree client is hurt but argue your case is only worth $40,000 total. If you already got $25,000 from the BI, they’ll offer only $15,000. MMI (Maximum Medical Improvement) They may wait to settle until you have finished all treatment to ensure no more bills are coming. Subrogation & Liens Client’s health insurance or a medical provider may have a \"lien\" on your settlement. Your attorney must negotiate these down so you keep more of the check. Case Phases : UM Settlement"},"13":{"t":"The Final Payout Process","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"The UM Release The Final Payout Process Once Cms agree on a number, the closing steps are: Once Cms agree on a number, the closing steps are: You will have the client sign a document releasing their insurance company from further liability for this specific accident. The Check The insurer issues a check, usually made out to both you and your law firm. Disbursement The funds are placed in a trust account to pay medical liens, legal fees, and issue the final \"net\" check to the client. Note: Because this is a first-party claim, if your insurer acts unreasonably (e.g., ignoring evidence or refusing to communicate), you may have grounds for a Bad Faith claim, which can sometimes result in compensation above your policy limits. Case Phases : UM Settlement"},"14":{"t":"Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"The Threshold & Coverage Documents that Matter The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The Insurance Declarations Page (\"Dec Page\"): Verifies that the client actually has UM/UIM coverage on their policy and establishes the upper limit of what can be demanded (e.g., John Doe's $100,000 policy limit). Case Phases : UM Settlement Proof of Uninsured Status: Documents establishing that the at-fault driver had no insurance. This can include a DMV letter of suspension, an official insurance denial letter from the tortfeasor's supposed carrier, or a police report explicitly noting the driver fled the scene (\"phantom vehicle\"). Third-Party Policy Exhaustion Ledger (For UIM Claims Only): If it is an Underinsured claim, you must provide a copy of the check or formal release proving you have already exhausted the at-fault driver's lower, underlying liability policy limits."},"15":{"t":"Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"Liability & Causation Documents that Matter The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. Official Police Accident Report: The baseline document establishing the mechanics of the crash, citations issued to the uninsured driver, and initial scene observations. Case Phases : UM Settlement Scene and Property Damage Photographs: Visual evidence showing the severity of the impact. High structural damage (like frame bending or deployment of airbags) helps defeat an adjuster's argument that the impact was too minor to cause severe bodily injury. Witness Statements / Affidavits: Neutral third-party accounts supporting your version of liability, highly critical in phantom vehicle/hit-and-run cases."},"16":{"t":"Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"Special Damages Documents (Economic Hard Costs) Documents that Matter The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. Itemized Medical Billing Statements: Complete, line-item bills from every provider (hospitals, imaging centers, chiropractors, etc.) to calculate the exact total of medical specials. Case Phases : UM Settlement Wage Loss Verification (WLV): A formal document completed by the client's employer confirming missed time from work, hourly pay rates, and total lost earnings resulting directly from the accident. Prior Lien Ledgers: Up-to-date statements from health insurance providers or subrogation companies (like Medicare or private health plans) detailing what has already been paid out, which is necessary for formatting your final net distribution calculations."},"17":{"t":"Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"General Damages Documents (Non-Economic Value) Documents that Matter The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. Diagnostic Medical Reports (MRI, CT, X-Ray): Objective diagnostic data that conclusively proves internal, physiological injuries (such as an acute disc herniation). Case Phases : UM Settlement Expert Medical Narrative & Permanency Rating: A definitive summary from the treating physician (e.g., Dr. Spine) outlining future care needs, explicit causation linking the injury to the crash, and; A formal Whole Person Impairment (WPI) rating. This is the single biggest weapon to counter \"pre- existing condition\" defenses."},"18":{"t":"Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : UM Settlement","w":"Final Pleading & Release Documents (Closing Phase) Documents that Matter The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The UM/UIM Settlement Phase needs documentation on coverage eligibility, the other driver’s liability, and damage valuation. Key documents for a strong UM Settlement/Demand Package are organized by function. The Formal UM Demand Letter: The comprehensive legal argument tying all facts, medical diagnostics, and case law together, concluding with a formal, time-sensitive financial demand (e.g., a 10-day limit to tender policy limits before triggering bad-faith). Case Phases : UM Settlement UM Release and Trust Agreement: The final contract provided by the insurer once a settlement is reached. It states that in exchange for the settlement funds, the client releases their own insurance company from further liability and agrees to protect the insurer's rights if the uninsured motorist is ever sued in the future."},"19":{"t":"Group Discussion: The Bad-Faith Arbitrage & The Permanent Deficit","s":"Skill Building : UM Settlement","w":"Group Discussion: The Bad-Faith Arbitrage & The Permanent Deficit The Scenario Liability is clear: John Doe was hit by a completely uninsured motorist. Liability is clear: John Doe was hit by a completely uninsured motorist. You are pursuing a claim under John’s own $100,000 UM Policy Limit. You are pursuing a claim under John’s own $100,000 UM Policy Limit. The Scenario Liability is clear: John Doe was hit by a completely uninsured motorist. You are pursuing a claim under John’s own $100,000 UM Policy Limit. Defense Tactics: Leveraged a 2018 lumbar strain and a 14-day treatment gap to offer a low $42,000. The Pressure: The adjuster demands verbal acceptance of $42,000 right now or they will permanently revoke the offer and force a lengthy arbitration. The Twist: Dr. Spine’s final report just arrived, confirming an acute L4-L5 herniation, a 5% Whole Person Impairment (WPI) rating, and permanent nerve damage caused entirely by the crash. Skill Building : UM Settlement Discuss your Step by Step Action Plan"},"20":{"t":"COMMON TYPES OF LIENS","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Lien Type Description Negotiability Medical Providers Doctors or hospitals who treated you on a \"letter of protection\" (LOP). High. Often willing to take a reduction to get paid immediately. Health Insurance Private insurers (BlueCross, Kaiser, etc.) seeking subrogation. Moderate. Subject to \"Made Whole\" or \"Common Fund\" doctrines. Medicare/Medicaid Government-funded programs. Low. Very strict federal/state formulas; takes a long time to process. ERISA Plans Self-funded employer health plans. Very Low. These are governed by federal law and are notoriously difficult to reduce. Workers' Comp If the injury happened while on the job. Moderate. Depends on whether there is a third-party settlement. COMMON TYPES OF LIENS Not all liens are created equal. Some have stronger legal backing (statutory) than others (contractual). Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction"},"21":{"t":"KEY LEGAL DOCTRINES USED FOR REDUCTION","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction Attorneys use specific legal arguments to force lienholders to take less than the full amount. This argues that since the attorney did all the work to secure This argues that since the attorney did all the work to secure the settlement, the lienholder should contribute to the legal fees. fees. This argues that since the attorney did all the work to secure the settlement, the lienholder should contribute to the legal fees. The \"Made Whole\" Doctrine In many jurisdictions, a lienholder cannot collect anything unless the plaintiff has been \"made whole\" (fully compensated for all damages, including pain and suffering). compensated for all damages, including pain and suffering). In many jurisdictions, a lienholder cannot collect anything unless the plaintiff has been \"made whole\" (fully compensated for all damages, including pain and suffering). The Common Fund Doctrine KEY LEGAL DOCTRINES USED FOR REDUCTION Comparative Fault If the settlement was reduced because the plaintiff was 20% at fault, the attorney will argue the lien should also be reduced by 20%. also be reduced by 20%. If the settlement was reduced because the plaintiff was 20% at fault, the attorney will argue the lien should also be reduced by 20%."},"22":{"t":"QUANTUM MERUIT PRINCIPLE - PRIOR","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction Quantum Meruit, meaning \"as much as he has deserved,\" is a legal doctrine used to assess the reasonable value of services when no valid contract exists, such as when a lawyer is dismissed before a case settles. Most Personal Injury cases use a contingency fee basis (e.g., 33%). If an attorney Most Personal Injury cases use a contingency fee basis (e.g., 33%). If an attorney is dismissed before winning the case, they cannot claim that fee. Instead, they is dismissed before winning the case, they cannot claim that fee. Instead, they file a Quantum Meruit lien to receive payment for the work completed while on the file a Quantum Meruit lien to receive payment for the work completed while on the case. The law seeks to balance two things: case. The law seeks to balance two things: Most Personal Injury cases use a contingency fee basis (e.g., 33%). If an attorney is dismissed before winning the case, they cannot claim that fee. Instead, they file a Quantum Meruit lien to receive payment for the work completed while on the case. The law seeks to balance two things: A client has an absolute right to fire their lawyer at any A client has an absolute right to fire their lawyer at any time. time. A client has an absolute right to fire their lawyer at any time. The Lawyer's Right A lawyer should be compensated for the benefit they provided to the case before being let go. provided to the case before being let go. A lawyer should be compensated for the benefit they provided to the case before being let go. The Client's Right QUANTUM MERUIT PRINCI"},"23":{"t":"INCREASING THE CLIENT'S NET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"INCREASING THE CLIENT'S NET A Case Manager’s job is 50% getting money from the insurance and 50% keeping it from the providers. The \"Pro-Rata\" The \"Pro-Rata\" Argument Tell providers: \"The total settlement is $X. If you don't reduce Tell providers: \"The total settlement is $X. If you don't reduce your bill by 30%, the client will refuse the settlement, and no your bill by 30%, the client will refuse the settlement, and no one—including you—gets paid for years.\" one—including you—gets paid for years.\" Tell providers: \"The total settlement is $X. If you don't reduce your bill by 30%, the client will refuse the settlement, and no one—including you—gets paid for years.\" (If applicable in your state): Argue that because the (If applicable in your state): Argue that because the client wasn't \"made whole\" (didn't get millions for their pain), the subrogation (health insurance) must reduc pain), the subrogation (health insurance) must reduce their claim significantly. their claim significantly. (If applicable in your state): Argue that because the client wasn't \"made whole\" (didn't get millions for their pain), the subrogation (health insurance) must reduce their claim significantly. The Final Payoff Letter Never send a check without a signed letter stating: Never send a check without a signed letter stating: \"Acceptance of this check constitutes full and final \"Acceptance of this check constitutes full and final satisfaction of all liens.\" satisfaction of all liens.\" Never send a check without a signed letter stating: \"Acceptance of this check constitutes full and final satisfactio"},"24":{"t":"AUDITING THE BILLING","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction Before you ask for a discount, make sure the bill is accurate. Many providers engage in \"Upcoding\" or \"Unbundling.\" Compare the medical records to the billing statement. If the records show a \"Level 3\" office visit but they billed for a records show a \"Level 3\" office visit but they billed for a \"Level 5\" (complex), you should \"Level 5\" (complex), you shouldn't be asking for a favor—you n't be asking for a favor—you should be demanding a correction. should be demanding a correction. Compare the medical records to the billing statement. If the records show a \"Level 3\" office visit but they billed for a \"Level 5\" (complex), you shouldn't be asking for a favor—you should be demanding a correction. Duplicate Charges Look for overlapping dates, especially between hospital stays and individual physician groups. and individual physician groups. Look for overlapping dates, especially between hospital stays and individual physician groups. Cross- Cross- Reference AUDITING THE BILLING Leverage the \"Common Fund\" Doctrine This is your key leverage, asserting to the lienholder that the Attorney This is your key leverage, asserting to the lienholder that the Attorney undertook the legal effort and incurred costs for the money they benefit from. undertook the legal effort and incurred costs for the money they benefit from. According to the 1/3 Rule, lienholders in many states must reduce their claims According to the 1/3 Rule, lienholders in many states must reduce their claims by their proportional share of your attorney’s fees and litigation cost"},"25":{"t":"THE NET SHEET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction The Ultimate Tool for Transparency, Case Control, and Maximizing Client Recovery Avoiding the Double-Dipping Trap: Avoiding the Double-Dipping Trap: Insurance companies will audit our files closely. We must clearly demonstrate Insurance companies will audit our files closely. We must clearly demonstrate where every dollar originated so medical lie where every dollar originated so medical liens are paid out correctly without ns are paid out correctly without cross-contaminating funds. cross-contaminating funds. Avoiding the Double-Dipping Trap: Insurance companies will audit our files closely. We must clearly demonstrate where every dollar originated so medical liens are paid out correctly without cross-contaminating funds. Maximizing Fee Structural Transparency: Attorney fee structures can vary depending on whether a policy is third-party BI or first-party vary depending on whether a policy is third-party BI or first-party UM/UIM. Tracking them separately keeps our financial ledgers ironclad. UM/UIM. Tracking them separately keeps our financial ledgers ironclad. Maximizing Fee Structural Transparency: Attorney fee structures can vary depending on whether a policy is third-party BI or first-party UM/UIM. Tracking them separately keeps our financial ledgers ironclad. The Purpose of a Combined BI/UM Net Sheet THE NET SHEET Protecting the Final Aggregate Net: By showing both recovery streams side by Protecting the Final Aggregate Net: By showing both recovery streams side by side, you show the client the compounding power of their covera"},"26":{"t":"THE NET SHEET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction The Ultimate Tool for Transparency, Case Control, and Maximizing Client Recovery Source: The at-fault driver’s insurance company (e.g., GEICO, Source: The at-fault driver’s insurance company (e.g., GEICO, Progressive). Progressive). CM Focus: Exhausting these policy limits first is usually a legal CM Focus: Exhausting these policy limits first is usually a legal prerequisite before you can even touch the first-party policy. prerequisite before you can even touch the first-party policy. Source: The at-fault driver’s insurance company (e.g., GEICO, Progressive). CM Focus: Exhausting these policy limits first is usually a legal prerequisite before you can even touch the first-party policy. Stream 1: Third-Party Bodily Injury (BI) Stream 1: Third-Party Bodily Injury (BI) THE NET SHEET Source: Our client’s own insurance policy (or a qualifying resident relative's Source: Our client’s own insurance policy (or a qualifying resident relative's household policy). household policy). CM Focus: Triggers only when the BI policy is completely exhausted or if the CM Focus: Triggers only when the BI policy is completely exhausted or if the tortfeasor is entirely uninsured. tortfeasor is entirely uninsured. Source: Our client’s own insurance policy (or a qualifying resident relative's household policy). CM Focus: Triggers only when the BI policy is completely exhausted or if the tortfeasor is entirely uninsured. Stream 2: First-Party Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist (UM/UIM) Motorist (UM/UIM) Stream 2: First-Party Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist (UM/U"},"27":{"t":"THE NET SHEET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction The BI/UM Master Financial Ledger THE NET SHEET"},"28":{"t":"THE NET SHEET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction Case Manager Protocols for Dual Settlements THE NET SHEET The Process: The Process: Secure the BI Tender First: Obtain the formal, written policy limits tender from the Secure the BI Tender First: Obtain the formal, written policy limits tender from the third-party carrier. third-party carrier. Execute the UIM Consent Protocol: Before signing the BI release, issue a formal Execute the UIM Consent Protocol: Before signing the BI release, issue a formal Consent to Settle notice to the UM/UIM carrier to protect subrogation rights. Consent to Settle notice to the UM/UIM carrier to protect subrogation rights. Consolidate the Medical Reductions: Negotiate medical bills globally against the combined $50,000.00 global value, rather than dealing with providers piecemeal for combined $50,000.00 global value, rather than dealing with providers piecemeal for each policy. each policy. Run the Aggregate Math: Fill out both sides of the net sheet simultaneously to verify Run the Aggregate Math: Fill out both sides of the net sheet simultaneously to verify that the final client net distribution matches our software ledger perfectly. that the final client net distribution matches our software ledger perfectly. The Process: Secure the BI Tender First: Obtain the formal, written policy limits tender from the third-party carrier. Execute the UIM Consent Protocol: Before signing the BI release, issue a formal Consent to Settle notice to the UM/UIM carrier to protect subrogation rights. Consolidate the Medical Reductions: Negotiate medical bills globally "},"29":{"t":"THE NET SHEET","s":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Case Phase : Lien Negotiations/Reduction Key Takeaway – The Value of a CM THE NET SHEET TTHE CORE RULE: TTHE CORE RULE: Yo Your job as a Case Manager isn't just data entry; it is financial engineering. When you ur job as a Case Manager isn't just data entry; it is financial engineering. When you combine a third-party BI recovery with a first-party UM household policy, you are combine a third-party BI recovery with a first-party UM household policy, you are doubling the client's recovery pool. Tracking these streams cleanly on a dual net sheet doubling the client's recovery pool. Tracking these streams cleanly on a dual net sheet ensures the firm is protected, the medical lines are clear, and the client receives every ensures the firm is protected, the medical lines are clear, and the client receives every single dollar they legally deserve! single dollar they legally deserve! TTHE CORE RULE: Your job as a Case Manager isn't just data entry; it is financial engineering. When you combine a third-party BI recovery with a first-party UM household policy, you are doubling the client's recovery pool. Tracking these streams cleanly on a dual net sheet ensures the firm is protected, the medical lines are clear, and the client receives every single dollar they legally deserve! TThe Co TThe Concept: ncept: Firm costs (police report fees, medical records retrieval, expert witness retainers) Firm costs (police report fees, medical records retrieval, expert witness retainers) must be deducted accurately. For clean accounting, costs are summed globally and must be deducted accurately. Fo"},"30":{"t":"The \"Doe v. Apex\" Final Net Challenge 🛑 The \"Doe v. Apex\" Final Net Challenge","s":"Skill Building: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","w":"Skill Building: Lien Negotiations/Reduction Scenario: Scenario: The \"Gross\" settlement is $50,000.00 (a hypothetical Settlement for Apex's commercial van), No UM Settlement. commercial van), No UM Settlement. Scenario: The \"Gross\" settlement is $50,000.00 (a hypothetical Settlement for Apex's commercial van), No UM Settlement. 🛑 The \"Doe v. Apex\" Final Net Challenge 🛑 The \"Doe v. Apex\" Final Net Challenge Your Tasks: Your Tasks: You have 15 minutes to \"find\" an extra $3,000 for the client using the existing case data. You have 15 minutes to \"find\" an extra $3,000 for the client using the existing case data. You have 15 minutes to \"find\" an extra $3,000 for the client using the existing case data. You have 15 minutes to \"find\" an extra $3,000 for the client using the existing case data. Draft a \"Zero-Recovery\" letter to BlueCross, using lien doctrines. Draft a \"Zero-Recovery\" letter to BlueCross, using lien doctrines. Create a Net Sheet for the client showing: Create a Net Sheet for the client showing: Reduced Expenses Amount Final Take Home after reduction Reasoning as to how did you obtain the indicated reduction amount (eg. Doctrines used) Reasoning as to how did you obtain the indicated reduction amount (eg. Doctrines used) Reasoning as to how did you obtain the indicated reduction amount (eg. Doctrines used) Reduced Expenses Amount Final Take Home after reduction Reasoning as to how did you obtain the indicated reduction amount (eg. Doctrines used)"},"31":{"t":"Core Objectives","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Compliance Fraud Prevention Compliance Case Phase: Disbursement Accuracy Verifying payment amounts against approved budgets. Ensuring all local, state, and federal regulations are met. Identifying red flags before funds leave the account. Ensuring the case meets statutory requirements (like the Statute of Limitations) before the demand is issued. Core Objectives"},"32":{"t":"The Workflow","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Documentation Authorization Reconciliation Case Phase: Disbursement Verification Reviewing \"Ready for Payment\" files. Sign-off for release. Confirming receipt of funds. The Workflow Collecting invoices/receipts. Confirm all \"Conditions Precedent\" are met. Ensure documents are legible and match the claim. Submit the packet to the finance/accounting team. Update the case file to reflect \"Paid\" status."},"33":{"t":"Documentation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Payment Request Form Verified Invoices Banking Information Case Phase: Disbursement Proof of Identity Documentation Checklist Valid ID of the payee. Signed by the beneficiary or authorized representative. Must include dates, service descriptions, and tax IDs. Update the case file to reflect \"Paid\" status. W-9/Tax Forms If applicable for vendor payments."},"34":{"t":"Common Challenges & Mitigation","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Change of Circumstance Duplicate Payments Case Phase: Disbursement Incomplete Paperwork Common Challenges & Mitigation Use a \"Kickback Protocol\"—clearly tell the client exactly what is missing and provide a deadline. Signed by the beneficiary or authorized representative. Cross-reference invoice numbers in the system before hitting \"Approve.\""},"35":{"t":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Transaction IDs Zero-Balance Confirmation Case Phase: Disbursement Net Sheet (The \"Money Trail\") Net Sheet (The \"Money Trail\") The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist A finalized accounting of every dollar that moved. Bank confirmation numbers for each payment. A report showing that the \"Approved Budget\" - \"Total Disbursed\" =0, indicating a de-obligated remaining balance. Before a case is marked \"Archived,\" the Case Manager must verify the presence of these four critical document sets: Itemized List Every check, ACH, or wire transfer."},"36":{"t":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Final Inspection/Affidavits Provider Lien Waivers Case Phase: Disbursement Proof of Delivery/Completion The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist Evidence that the funds were used for their intended purpose. If the disbursement was for construction or repairs, a signed \"Certificate of Completion\" or a final inspection report is required. If paying providers, a signed document stating they have been paid in full and waive any future claims against the client or the claim. Before a case is marked \"Archived,\" the Case Manager must verify the presence of these four critical document sets: Signed Acknowledgment of Receipt The client’s signature confirming they received the funds."},"37":{"t":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Duplication of Benefits Final Review Case Phase: Disbursement Compliance & Identity Verification The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist A signed statement from the beneficiary confirming they didn't receive \"double-funding\" from another source (like insurance) after the case started. Before a case is marked \"Archived,\" the Case Manager must verify the presence of these four critical document sets: Updated W-9/Tax Documents Ensuring the most recent tax info is on file for any 1099 reporting."},"38":{"t":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Discrepancy Notes Case Phase: Disbursement The Internal \"Closing Memo\" The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist If the final payout was different from the initial estimate, explain why here (e.g., \"Change order approved on March 12th due to unforeseen site conditions\"). Before a case is marked \"Archived,\" the Case Manager must verify the presence of these four critical document sets: Case Summary A brief \"How it started vs. How it ended.\" Often overlooked, this is a one-page summary written by the Case Manager for the internal file. It should include: Exception Reports Documentation of any policy waivers granted during the process."},"39":{"t":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","s":"Case Phase: Disbursement","w":"Document Type Retention Period Action After Period Financial Records 7 Years (Standard) Digital Deletion Eligibility Docs 3–5 Years Archival / Off-site storage Correspondence 3 Years Purge from active server Case Phase: Disbursement Retention & Disposition Schedule The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist Before a case is marked \"Archived,\" the Case Manager must verify the presence of these four critical document sets: Closing paperwork must be organized according to a Retention Policy."},"40":{"t":"John Doe v. Apex Delivery Final Tasks","s":"Skill Building - Closing a Case","w":"Skill Building - Closing a Case Scenario John Doe v. Apex Delivery Final Tasks Using the John Doe v. Apex Delivery case file , let’s apply the closing concepts we’ve discussed. John Doe (the plaintiff) was injured by an Apex Delivery vehicle. The case has reached a settlement of $150,000, and we are now moving through the final disbursement and closing paperwork. Reconcile the gross settlement against all \"liens\" and costs. Ensure that the case is “Audit Ready”. Create a closing letter. Tasks After sending the closing letter to John Doe, you receive a new medical bill from a \"Radiology Imaging Center\" amounting to $1,200, linked to the Apex accident. This charge was not recorded in your ledger. Upon discussing this with the client, he expressed significant frustration. What steps should you take next? Who is accountable for settling this bill? Plot Twist"},"41":{"t":"Thank You!","s":"Thank You!","w":"Thank You!"}},"open":[1,2],"close":[41],"topics":[{"h":"UM Demand: Why This Phase Matters and the Strategy","section":"Case Phase: UM Demand","pages":[3,4]},{"h":"BI Exhaustion and Consent","section":"Case Phase: UM Demand","pages":[5]},{"h":"The UM Demand Package","section":"Case Phase: UM Demand","pages":[6,7,8]},{"h":"The UM Settlement: Negotiation and Settlement","section":"Case Phase: UM Settlement","pages":[9,10,11]},{"h":"UM Settlement Challenges","section":"Case Phase: UM Settlement","pages":[12]},{"h":"The Final Payout Process","section":"Case Phase: UM Settlement","pages":[13]},{"h":"Documents That Matter","section":"Case Phase: UM Settlement","pages":[14,15,16,17,18]},{"h":"Skill Building: UM Settlement","section":"Case Phase: UM Settlement","pages":[19]},{"h":"Common Types of Liens","section":"Case Phase: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","pages":[20]},{"h":"Key Legal Doctrines for Reduction and Quantum Meruit","section":"Case Phase: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","pages":[21,22]},{"h":"Increasing the Client's Net and Auditing the Billing","section":"Case Phase: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","pages":[23,24]},{"h":"The Net Sheet","section":"Case Phase: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","pages":[25,26,27,28,29]},{"h":"Skill Building: the \"Doe v. Apex\" Final Net Challenge","section":"Case Phase: Lien Negotiations/Reduction","pages":[30]},{"h":"Disbursement: Core Objectives and the Workflow","section":"Case Phase: Disbursement","pages":[31,32]},{"h":"Documentation Checklist and Common Challenges","section":"Case Phase: Disbursement","pages":[33,34]},{"h":"The Final Case Reconciliation Checklist","section":"Case Phase: Disbursement","pages":[35,36,37,38,39]},{"h":"Skill Building: Closing a Case","section":"Case Phase: Disbursement","pages":[40]}],"w":1920,"h":1080},"4":{"pages":{"1":{"t":"Day 4","s":"Case Management","w":"Case Management Training Day 4"},"2":{"t":"Case Phase: Mediation Case Phase: Arbitration","s":"Training Agenda","w":"Case Phase: Mediation Case Phase: Arbitration 02 Training Agenda 01 02 03 Common Bottlenecks in Mediation and Arbitration"},"3":{"t":"Subject-Matter Expertise & Background","s":"Introduction","w":"Mediator Profile Introduction Introducing the Neutral – Mediator vs. Arbitrator Experienced personal injury trial attorneys or retired judges specializing in negotiation dynamics, lien resolution, and realistic case valuations. Arbitrator Profile Retired judges or senior trial attorneys with specialized mastery of evidentiary rules, tort law, liability standards, and medical causation. Subject-Matter Expertise & Background"},"4":{"t":"Assignment & Selection Workflows","s":"Introduction","w":"Selecting a Mediator: Introduction Introducing the Neutral – Mediator vs. Arbitrator Voluntary / Mutual Selection: Both parties review panel resumes and agree on a mediator known for fair evaluations. Court Appointment: If ordered by a judge, selected from a court-approved neutral roster. Selecting an Arbitrator: Contract / Policy Mandate: Required by UM/UIM policy contracts or post-mediation stipulations. Panel Strike List (AAA / JAMS): Dispute resolution bodies provide a list of candidates; both sides vet background, rank preferences, and \"strike\" unwanted names until a neutral is appointed. Assignment & Selection Workflows"},"5":{"t":"Extent of Decision-Making Power","s":"Introduction","w":"Mediator Power (Zero Decision Power) Introduction Introducing the Neutral – Mediator vs. Arbitrator Role: Facilitator & Negotiator. Decision Authority: Zero. Cannot force a settlement, render a ruling, or order payout. Parties retain 100% control over whether to sign. Arbitrator Power (Absolute Decision Power) Role: Private Judge & Finder of Fact. Decision Authority: Total. Issues a legally binding Arbitration Award. Can determine liability, award financial damages, and exclude evidence. Appeal rights are virtually non-existent under the law. Extent of Decision-Making Power"},"6":{"t":"Mediation Logistics & Scheduling","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"Unified Availability Case Phases : Mediation Protocols for CMs Proactively align complex calendars. Gather 3-4 workable windows from the client, handling attorneys, and opposing counsel before initiating neutral scheduling. Environmental Control Reserve secure physical conference rooms with client waiting areas, or configure stable Zoom links with private virtual breakout rooms to protect strategy sessions. Mediation Logistics & Scheduling Facilitating compromise, setting logistics, and managing confidential negotiations. Facilitating compromise, setting logistics, and managing confidential negotiations."},"7":{"t":"Preparing Mediation Binders","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"Organized Case Summaries Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling A Mediation Binder (sometimes called a Mediation Packet) is a highly organized, comprehensive compilation of the most critical documents in a case. Preparing Mediation Binders Think of it as the ultimate \"cheat sheet\" and reference guide for the handling attorney and the mediator. During a fast-paced mediation session, the attorney cannot afford to fumble through hundreds of loose files or dig around on a laptop. They need to find a specific medical record, contract clause, or deposition quote within five seconds to counter an opponent's argument."},"8":{"t":"Preparing Mediation Binders","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"Organized Case Summaries Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling Mediation packets are the roadmap for settlement. Compile crucial medical records, verified special damages sheets, liability reports, and pivotal case exhibits. Meticulous indexing and bookmarking ensure the handling attorney and mediator can access critical evidence in seconds during fast-paced compromise sessions. Preparing Mediation Binders"},"9":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 1. Executive Summary & Administrative Details","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling This is the first thing the attorney sees when opening the binder. It establishes the basic framework. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 1. Executive Summary & Administrative Details Key documents include: Case CM Snapshot/Summary Sheet: A 1-page overview of parties, firm file numbers, and opposing counsel contact details. Mediation Order/Agreement: A formal court order for mediation or a private contract outlining rules and fee splits. The Schedule: A log detailing the date, start time, location (or Zoom link/breakout room), and mediator's name."},"10":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 2. The Mediation Briefs","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling These documents outline the legal and factual arguments that both sides are bringing to the table. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 2. The Mediation Briefs Our Confidential Mediation Brief: The comprehensive statement prepared by our handling attorney that outlines our strongest arguments, key evidence, and settlement positions. Opposing Counsel’s Mediation Brief: The brief sent over by the other side. This allows our attorney to read the opponent’s arguments side-by-side to prepare rebuttals."},"11":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 3. Core Pleadings (The Legal Framework)","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling The formal documents filed with the court that dictate what the lawsuit is actually about. The Active Complaint: The operative complaint detailing our client's allegations. The Answer / Affirmative Defenses: The opposing party's formal response, including any legal defenses they are using to shield themselves from liability. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 3. Core Pleadings (The Legal Framework)"},"12":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 4. Key Evidence & Liability Exhibits","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling The \"smoking guns.\" This section contains the raw evidence proving that our side is right. Contracts or Key Correspondence: In business or contract disputes, the specific pages containing disputed clauses, or critical email threads where promises were made/broken. Police, Incident, or Accident Reports: In personal injury cases, the official government or store reports detailing how the incident occurred. Photographs & Video Stills: Visual proof of property damage, scene layouts, or physical injuries. Deposition Summaries: High-level summaries of testimony given by key witnesses, along with the exact, highlighted transcript pages for quick cross-referencing. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 4. Key Evidence & Liability Exhibits"},"13":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 5. Damages, Financials, & Expert Reports","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling This section justifies the dollar amount we are asking for. Medical Records Summary & Key Bills: Chronological medical chart notes and a ledger totaling all medical expenses incurred to date. Proof of Financial Loss: Tax returns, pay stubs, or profit-and-loss statements proving lost wages or business disruption. Expert Witness Reports: Summaries or formal declarations from retained experts (e.g., accident reconstructionists, medical experts, economic analysts) validating our claims. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 5. Damages, Financials, & Expert Reports"},"14":{"t":"What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 6. Settlement History & Draft Agreements","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"A gold-standard mediation binder is generally divided into 6 key sections. Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling The closing section that prepares the attorney to finalize the deal. The Negotiation Log: A chronological list of every demand our firm has made and every counter- offer received from the defense, preventing any confusion over the current baseline number. Draft Settlement Agreement / Release Template: A boilerplate or pre-drafted settlement terms sheet. If the parties agree on a number, the attorney can instantly pull this out to get signatures before anyone leaves the room. What Documents Go Into a Mediation Binder? 6. Settlement History & Draft Agreements Case Manager Pro-Tip: Prepare three binders for in-person mediation—one for the attorney, one for the mediator, and one for the client. For virtual mediation, ensure the PDF binder is OCR-scanned for easy keyword searching during negotiations."},"15":{"t":"Quality Assurance and Logistics Auditor","s":"Case Phases : Mediation","w":"Case Phases : Mediation Logistics & Scheduling Case Managers ensures that the handling attorney is completely armed and organized before the mediation session. Quality Assurance and Logistics Auditor Binder Multi-Sets: Build three identical physical copies of the binder or create a unified digital environment for the Handling Attorney, Neutral Mediator, and Client. Searchability Optimization: For digital binders, perform a deep text-recognition scan (OCR) to enable quick evidence searches using Ctrl + F. Systematic Indexing: Integrate six numbered divider tabs that align with the firm's master index framework."},"16":{"t":"CREATE YOUR MEDIATION BINDERS","s":"Skill Building: Mediation","w":"Skill Building: Mediation CREATE YOUR MEDIATION BINDERS 🚨 Prepare a Complete Comprehensive Mediation Binders for your case and submit to the Attorney for Approval. Sample Mediation Brief"},"17":{"t":"📑 THE LSH CRITICAL THINKING CHALLENGE: THE PRE-MEDIATION AUDIT","s":"Skill Building: Mediation","w":"Skill Building: Mediation 📑 THE LSH CRITICAL THINKING CHALLENGE: THE PRE-MEDIATION AUDIT Setting: It is June 18, 2026. You are exactly 48 hours away from the hard June 18, 2026 Mediation Completion Deadline. The lead attorney is stuck in court and asks you to run a final compliance audit using the primary file data to ensure we are ready to negotiate. 🚨 THE CRISIS SCENARIO You open the digital file and find a series of conflicting data entries, missing documents, and aggressive pushback from the defense counsel, Jane Vance. To pass this challenge, you cannot just check boxes; you must investigate the raw data records from John Doe Case File to solve these 2 critical landmines before the attorney steps into the mediation room."},"18":{"t":"📑 THE LSH CRITICAL THINKING CHALLENGE: THE PRE-MEDIATION AUDIT","s":"Skill Building: Mediation","w":"Skill Building: Mediation 📑 THE LSH CRITICAL THINKING CHALLENGE: THE PRE-MEDIATION AUDIT Test your CM Critical Thinking Skills! Problem 1: The defense attorney calls and states: \"We received your demand letter. However, your medical ledger is completely unverified, and we will not extend a real settlement offer at mediation while your damages are entirely unsubstantiated.\" Problem 2: Metro General Hospital has asserted a formal medical lien against any potential settlement for $45,000.00. Concurrently, BlueCross Recovery Services has filed an ERISA health insurance subrogation lien for $20,000.00."},"19":{"t":"THE ANATOMY OF A PERSONAL INJURY ARBITRATION","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"CSL vs. Split Limits: THE ANATOMY OF A PERSONAL INJURY ARBITRATION In Personal Injury (PI) practice, when a motor vehicle accident (MVA) or commercial In Personal Injury (PI) practice, when a motor vehicle accident (MVA) or commercial trucking case fails to settle at mediation, it is frequently routed to Arbitration. trucking case fails to settle at mediation, it is frequently routed to Arbitration. In Personal Injury (PI) practice, when a motor vehicle accident (MVA) or commercial trucking case fails to settle at mediation, it is frequently routed to Arbitration. Observe & Document: Take comprehensive notes on witness testimonies, opposing counsel’s focus areas, and the arbitrator's reactions to specific evidence. Log Directives: Immediately record any post-hearing brief timelines or evidentiary rulings issued by the arbitrator from the bench. Update the CMS: Upon returning to the office, instantly log new deadlines into the digital case profile and draft an internal summary memo for the primary handling attorney. Case Phases : Arbitration"},"20":{"t":"Operational Mindset","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"UNDERSTANDING THE ARBITRATION PHASE Operational Mindset Ohifting from a Compromise Focus (Mediation) to a Trial-Ready Standard (Arbitration). Ohifting from a Compromise Focus (Mediation) to a Trial-Ready Standard (Arbitration). Ohifting from a Compromise Focus (Mediation) to a Trial-Ready Standard (Arbitration). Unlike mediation—which is a voluntary, non-binding negotiation designed to reach a compromise— arbitration is an adversarial, formal hearing where a neutral third party (the Arbitrator) acts as the judge and issues a final, binding decision (the Award). Case Phases : Arbitration Once an arbitration award is issued, the right to appeal is drastically limited by law. Therefore, a case manager's operational execution in this phase must treat the upcoming arbitration hearing with the exact same meticulous precision as a multi-day trial in state or federal court."},"21":{"t":"Operational Tracks","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"1. Critical Timelines & Calendar Hard Rules Operational Tracks Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. The moment a case is routed to the arbitration track, you must hard-code and actively monitor these specific parameters on the firm's master calendar: Arbitration Hearing Date: The final trial-like date where the handling attorney will present legal arguments, cross-examine witnesses, and introduce evidence. Arbitration Brief Deadline: The absolute cutoff date to submit our finalized factual claims, legal arguments, exhibit indexes, and witness lists directly to the arbitrator and opposing counsel. Neutral Arbitrator Selection Cutoff: The deadline to review, vet, and strike names from the panel of potential arbitrators provided by bodies like the AAA (American Arbitration Association) or JAMS. Case Phases : Arbitration"},"22":{"t":"Operational Tracks","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"2. Logistics & Setup Protocols Operational Tracks Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. Your core responsibilities during the arbitration track are heavily operational and administrative, requiring intense timeline tracking, strict scheduling, and rigid document management. You serve as the primary logistics coordinator for setting up the arbitration framework: Coordinate Availability: Communicate directly with opposing staff to secure 3 to 4 mutually agreeable blocks of time before formal outreach to the neutral arbitrator. Retaining the Neutral: Vet the chosen arbitrator, confirm their specific daily and hourly fee schedule, and route the required retainer deposits to accounting for immediate payment. Logistics Management: Distribute comprehensive calendar invitations to all internal parties, and reserve the physical firm conference rooms or configure secure, multi-layered virtual Zoom room layouts with clear breakout parameters. Case Phases : Arbitration"},"23":{"t":"Evens a Boundaries - Can And Cannot do at Hearings","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"What You CAN Do at the Arbitration Hearing: Evens a Boundaries - Can And Cannot do at Hearings ⚠ THE GOLDEN RULE: THE GOLDEN RULE: Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. ⚠️ THE GOLDEN RULE: Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. Observe & Document: Take comprehensive notes on witness testimonies, opposing counsel’s focus areas, and the arbitrator's reactions to specific evidence. Log Directives: Immediately record any post-hearing brief timelines or evidentiary rulings issued by the arbitrator from the bench. Update the CMS: Upon returning to the office, instantly log new deadlines into the digital case profile "},"24":{"t":"Evens a Boundaries - Can And Cannot do at Hearings","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"What You CAN Do at the Arbitration Hearing: Evens a Boundaries - Can And Cannot do at Hearings ⚠ THE POLICY ON THE UNAUTHORIZED PRACTICE OF LAW (UPL) THE POLICY ON THE UNAUTHORIZED PRACTICE OF LAW (UPL) Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. ⚠️ THE POLICY ON THE UNAUTHORIZED PRACTICE OF LAW (UPL) Case Managers are strictly prohibited from presenting legal arguments, speaking on the record, or representing clients directly in front of an arbitrator. Doing so constitutes the Unauthorized Practice of Law (UPL). Your presence at an arbitration hearing is strictly limited to administrative assistance, technical support, and structural observation. Observe & Document: Take comprehensive notes on witness testimonies, opposing counsel’s focus areas, and the arbitrator's reactions to specific evidence. Log Directives: Immediately record any post-hearing brief timelines or evidentiary rulings issued by the arbitrator from the ben"},"25":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 1: THE ARBITRATION SUBMISSIONS This section contains the administrative and foundational paperwork that sets the ground rules for the entire proceeding. Key documents include: Case Manager Snapshot Sheet: A 1-page overview with internal file numbers, party designations, and contact details for counsel. Arbitration Brief: The final version of factual claims, liability calculations, and legal argume"},"26":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 2: CORE LITIGATION PLEADINGS The arbitrator needs a copy of the formal legal framework of the lawsuit. Operative Amended Complain: The active complaint outlining specific counts pursued; old complaints should be removed to prevent confusion. Answer & Affirmative Defenses: The defense's response explaining how they plan to avoid liability or argue comparative fault."},"27":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 3: LIABILITIES & BIOMECHANICAL EVIDENCE This section contains the raw evidence proving that the adverse driver is 100% at fault for the impact. Official Incident & Police Reports: Include unredacted law enforcement reports, officer narratives, diagrams, and citations from the scene. High-Resolution Color Exhibits: Require photos showing vehicle damage and scene layout; grayscale prints are not all"},"28":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 4: PROVING BODY ACCIDENT MEDICAL DAMAGES This section justifies the exact financial compensation being demanded for your client's physical injuries. Key Components of Medical Records: Consolidated Medical Ledger: A chronological treatment chart with an active billing spreadsheet tracking total operation costs. Comprehensive Provider Treatment Logs: Records, intake packets, chart notes, and invoice"},"29":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 5: THE PRIOR MEDICAL SHIELD Defense attorneys actively weaponize prior records to argue \"pre-existing degenerative conditions.\" This section is your proactive defense shield. Prior Injury Records & Work History Files: Document past injuries, such as John Doe's 2018 strain, which resolved in 4 weeks without pre- existing defects. Retained Expert Reports: Official statements from accident reconstruc"},"30":{"t":"Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter","s":"Case Phases : Arbitration","w":"If the Case Manager leaves out a page or prints a critical vehicle intrusion exhibit in low-quality grayscale, the Arbitrator can permanently exclude that evidence, destroying your client's case with zero chance of appeal. Arbitration Binders and Documents that Matter Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. Unlike mediation—where the goal is a compromise—an Arbitration Hearing is a binding legal battle. The Arbitration Binder is not just a reference packet; it is a formal, trial-ready evidentiary record submitted directly to a private judge. A high-performance personal injury firm structures an Arbitration Binder into 6 critical, tabbed sections. Here is the operational breakdown of the documents that actually matter: Case Phases : Arbitration SECTION 6: ECONOMIC LOSSES & LIEN RECONCILIATIONS The financial closing ledger that directly impacts the final cash payout to the client. Forensic Lost Wage Verifications: W-2 forms, individual tax returns, or employment verifications calculating accurate earnings loss and business disruption. The Chronological Negotiation Ledger: A master table mapping out the historical timeline of demands and counter-o"},"31":{"t":"THE PI CRITICAL THINKING AUDIT CHALLENGE","s":"Skill Building - Arbitration","w":"Investigate the raw data records in the case file to solve these 2 critical evidentiary landmines before the brief is finalized and locked in front of the arbitrator: THE PI CRITICAL THINKING AUDIT CHALLENGE CASE STUDY FILE: JOHN DOE V. APEX DELIVERY SERVICES & ROBERT W. SMITH CASE STUDY FILE: JOHN DOE V. APEX DELIVERY SERVICES & ROBERT W. SMITH CASE STUDY FILE: JOHN DOE V. APEX DELIVERY SERVICES & ROBERT W. SMITH The Defense Position: \"Plaintiff has a documented history of a Lumbar Strain from 2018. Because Plaintiff’s counsel has failed to provide a baseline pre-accident MRI, the Arbitrator must conclude that the current L4–L5 protrusions are a continuation of a pre-existing degenerative condition.\" Skill Building - Arbitration The case failed to settle at mediation, and you are exactly 48 hours away from the formal Arbitration Brief Deadline. The case failed to settle at mediation, and you are exactly 48 hours away from the formal Arbitration Brief Deadline. Defense Counsel Jane Vance has filed a Pre-Hearing Statement asserting that our client’s current back complaints are Defense Counsel Jane Vance has filed a Pre-Hearing Statement asserting that our client’s current back complaints are entirely a result of a legacy work injury from 2018, and that his claims of \"severe mental distress\" are completely entirely a result of a legacy work injury from 2018, and that his claims of \"severe mental distress\" are completely unverified by the medical timeline. unverified by the medical timeline. The case failed to settle at mediation, and you are exactly 48 hours away from the for"},"32":{"t":"The Arbitration Audit & Binder Build","s":"Skill Building: Arbitration","w":"The Arbitration Audit & Binder Build The Emergency Scenario Case File: John Doe v. Apex Delivery Services & Robert W. Smith Case File: John Doe v. Apex Delivery Services & Robert W. Smith The High-Pressure Crisis: The previous Case Manager missed logging the master The High-Pressure Crisis: The previous Case Manager missed logging the master schedule—the formal Arbitration Hearing is in 48 Hours! schedule—the formal Arbitration Hearing is in 48 Hours! Operational Mandate: Audit the scrambled file, sort 12 mixed documents into a 6-tab Operational Mandate: Audit the scrambled file, sort 12 mixed documents into a 6-tab trial binder, apply strict Quality Control (QC) rules, and resolve 2 defense landmines trial binder, apply strict Quality Control (QC) rules, and resolve 2 defense landmines before brief submission. before brief submission. The Emergency Scenario Case File: John Doe v. Apex Delivery Services & Robert W. Smith The High-Pressure Crisis: The previous Case Manager missed logging the master schedule—the formal Arbitration Hearing is in 48 Hours! Operational Mandate: Audit the scrambled file, sort 12 mixed documents into a 6-tab trial binder, apply strict Quality Control (QC) rules, and resolve 2 defense landmines before brief submission. Skill Building: Arbitration Upload your Binders in the CMS"},"33":{"t":"Mediation Bottlenecks (The Compromise Traps)","s":"Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration","w":"Mediation Bottlenecks (The Compromise Traps)Unverified Medical Ledgers & Liens: Unreconciled hospital bills or missing final treatment logs make damages look unsubstantiated to the defense. Missing Decision-Makers: Key insurance adjusters or defense representatives without full settlement authority attending without pre-negotiated parameters. Lack of Confidential Brief Alignment: Briefs delivered late or missing critical exhibits ($MRIs$, crash diagrams), stalling mediator preparation. Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration"},"34":{"t":"Arbitration Bottlenecks (The Trial-Ready Landmines)","s":"Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration","w":"Arbitration Bottlenecks (The Trial-Ready Landmines) Evidentiary Exclusions: Missing documents or low-quality black-and-white ph Unaddressed Pre-Existing Defense Traps: Failing to shield prior injury records (e.g., past strains) with expert medical declarations. Calendar & Deadline Misses: Unsynchronized scheduling windows for neutral arbitrators or missing hard brief submission cutoffs. Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration"},"35":{"t":"Case Manager Operational Countermeasures","s":"Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration","w":"Case Manager Operational Countermeasures 48-Hour Pre-Session Audit: Hard-code a mandatory file audit 48 hours before any deadline to verify ledgers and binder completeness. Common Bottlenecks in Mediation & Arbitration Proactive Lien Reconciliation: Audit health insurance Explanation of Benefits (EOBs) early to eliminate double-dipping hospital liens."},"36":{"t":"Thank You!","s":"Thank You!","w":"Thank You!"}},"open":[1,2],"close":[36],"topics":[{"h":"Mediators and Arbitrators: Expertise, Selection and Decision-Making Power","section":"Introduction","pages":[3,4,5]},{"h":"Mediation Logistics and Scheduling","section":"Case Phase: Mediation","pages":[6]},{"h":"Preparing Mediation Binders","section":"Case Phase: Mediation","pages":[7,8]},{"h":"What Goes Into a Mediation Binder","section":"Case Phase: Mediation","pages":[9,10,11,12,13,14]},{"h":"Quality Assurance and the Logistics Auditor","section":"Case Phase: Mediation","pages":[15]},{"h":"Skill Building: Mediation","section":"Case Phase: Mediation","pages":[16,17,18]},{"h":"The Anatomy of a PI Arbitration and the Operational Mindset","section":"Case Phase: Arbitration","pages":[19,20]},{"h":"Operational Tracks","section":"Case Phase: Arbitration","pages":[21,22]},{"h":"What You Can and Cannot Do at Hearings","section":"Case Phase: Arbitration","pages":[23,24]},{"h":"Arbitration Binders and Documents That Matter","section":"Case Phase: Arbitration","pages":[25,26,27,28,29,30]},{"h":"Skill Building: Arbitration","section":"Case Phase: Arbitration","pages":[31,32]},{"h":"Common Bottlenecks in Mediation and Arbitration","section":"Common Bottlenecks","pages":[33,34,35]}],"w":1920,"h":1080},"5":{"pages":{"1":{"t":"Day 5","s":"Case Management","w":"Case Management Training Day 5"},"2":{"t":"Case Phase: Litigation Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation","s":"Training Agenda","w":"Case Phase: Litigation Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation Liability Disputes ; MIA Clients 02 Training Agenda 01 02 03"},"3":{"t":"The Case Manager’s Tactical Role in Litigation The Litigation Lifecycle: Where You Fit In","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"The Litigation Lifecycle: Where You Fit In Case Phases : Litigation The Case Manager’s Tactical Role in Litigation A lawsuit is a highly structured, court-mandated process. If you miss a deadline or misfile a document, the court can dismiss the client's case entirely. When negotiations break down and a case moves into formal litigation, the dynamics change entirely. As a When negotiations break down and a case moves into formal litigation, the dynamics change entirely. As a case manager, it is essential to grasp how Property Damage (PD) relates to litigation. It can either act as the case manager, it is essential to grasp how Property Damage (PD) relates to litigation. It can either act as the definitive evidence of injury or become the defense's most formidable tool. definitive evidence of injury or become the defense's most formidable tool. When negotiations break down and a case moves into formal litigation, the dynamics change entirely. As a case manager, it is essential to grasp how Property Damage (PD) relates to litigation. It can either act as the definitive evidence of injury or become the defense's most formidable tool."},"4":{"t":"Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities Tracking the Statute of Limitations (SOL): This is the most critical deadline in the firm. You must ensure the Complaint is filed well before the SOL expires. Managing Process Servers: Once the attorney drafts the Complaint, you must coordinate with process servers or the local sheriff to have the defendant personally served. The \"Proof of Service\" Check: Track down the signed Affidavit of Service from the server and file it with the court immediately. If the defendant evades service, alert your attorney so they can file for alternative service (like publication or certified mail). Phase 1: The Pleadings & Service Stage Your primary job here is to ensure the lawsuit is successfully kicked off and that the defendant cannot claim they Your primary job here is to ensure the lawsuit is successfully kicked off and that the defendant cannot claim they \"never knew\" about it. \"never knew\" about it. Phase 1: The Pleadings & Service Stage Your primary job here is to ensure the lawsuit is successfully kicked off and that the defendant cannot claim they \"never knew\" about it."},"5":{"t":"Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities A. Answering Interrogatories & Requests for Production (RFP) The defense will send a long list of written questions (Interrogatories) and demands for physical documents (RFPs). Your Strategy: Do not just mail these to the client and tell them to fill it out. Schedule a dedicated \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" (either in person or via Zoom). The Execution: Walk the client through every question. Draft their answers into the formal legal template, and compile all requested documents (tax returns, employment records, past medical history). The Verification: Ensure the client signs a \"Verification Page\" under oath, swearing that the answers are true. Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers spend 80% of their litigation time. spend 80% of their litigation time. Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers spend 80% of their litigation time."},"6":{"t":"Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities B. Deposition Coordination & Preparation A deposition is an oral interview under oath, recorded by a court reporter. The defense attorney will cross- examine your client for hours to try and trip them up. Logistics Management: You are responsible for scheduling the court reporter, the videographer, booking the conference room (or setting up the Zoom link), and coordinating calendars between your attorney, the defense attorney, and the client. Client Hand-Holding: Clients are terrified of depositions. Send them a preparation packet one week prior. Remind them of the core rules: Listen carefully, pause before answering, never guess, and tell the absolute truth. Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers spend 80% of their litigation time. spend 80% of their litigation time. Phase 2: The Discovery Stage (The Heavy Lifting) Discovery is the formal process where both sides must lay their cards on the table. This is where case managers spend 80% of their litigation time."},"7":{"t":"Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities Updating Medical Records & Bills (The Final Run): Litigation can take 1 to 3 years. During that time, the client may have seen new doctors, undergone surgery, or accumulated massive new medical bills. You must update all records to ensure the jury sees the total cost of the injury. Subpoena Management: Draft and issue trial subpoenas to witnesses, treating physicians, and police officers to ensure they legally show up to testify. Trial Binder Organization: Assemble the trial binders containing all exhibits (medical records, scene photos, property damage estimates) meticulously indexed and tabbed for your attorney’s easy access at the counsel table. Phase 3: The Trial & Mediation Preparation Stage As the case nears its end, your job is to compile the final puzzle pieces so the attorney can walk into court fully As the case nears its end, your job is to compile the final puzzle pieces so the attorney can walk into court fully armed. armed. Phase 3: The Trial & Mediation Preparation Stage As the case nears its end, your job is to compile the final puzzle pieces so the attorney can walk into court fully armed."},"8":{"t":"The Litigation Mindset: Critical Traps to Avoid","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Mindset: Critical Traps to Avoid Trap 1: Treating Court Deadlines Like Insurance Deadlines In pre-lit, if an insurance adjuster gives you a deadline, you can usually ask for a polite extension. In litigation, court deadlines are absolute. Missing a discovery deadline can result in the judge blocking your client from using critical medical evidence at trial. Trap 2: Letting the Client Talk Directly to the Adjuster Once a lawsuit is filed, all communication with the defense must go through legal counsel. If an insurance adjuster or defense investigator tries to call your client directly, step in immediately, cease the communication, and notify your attorney. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) [ ] Service Check: Every new lawsuit must have a confirmed process server assigned within 48 hours of filing. [ ] Discovery Countdown: Initial discovery responses must be drafted and sent to the attorney for review at least 7 days before the court-mandated deadline. [ ] The 30-Day Client Pulse: Because litigation moves slowly, clients often feel forgotten. Call every litigation client at least once every 30 days just to give them a status update, even if nothing new has happened in court. To ensure you are managing your litigation docket effectively, audit your files weekly against these metrics:"},"9":{"t":"The Pre-Deposition Audit: What You Must Do First","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"The Pre-Deposition Audit: What You Must Do First Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. 1.The Pre-Deposition Audit: What You Must Do First Before scheduling a preparation meeting with the client, it’s essential to review the file thoroughly to pinpoint any “landmines” that the defense may exploit. Examine Previous Medical History: Investigate the intake notes and medical records for any prior injuries. For instance, if the client injured their lower back in 2018, make a note of it. The defense will inquire about this, and it’s essential for your client to respond truthfully while avoiding any impression of concealment. Audit Discovery Responses: Review the written interrogatories that the client has already signed. It's crucial that their oral testimony during the deposition aligns with their written answers. Consistency is key to establishing credibility. Review Social Media: Examine the client's public social media accounts. If they assert a severe knee injury but shared photos of themselves hiking or dancing last month, it's crucial that your attorney is informed right away."},"10":{"t":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\"","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. Pillar 1: De-escalating Anxiety 2. Overseeing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" When inviting the client in (or hosting a Zoom session) to get them ready, organize the meeting around these three essential pillars: The Script: “Mr. Jones, this is not a courtroom drama. There are no judges, no juries, and no shouting involved. It’s simply a business meeting in a conference room where the other party will hear your perspective. While you cannot lose your case today, you can safeguard it by maintaining your composure Clients often feel anxious as their understanding of legal matters is shaped by what they see on television."},"11":{"t":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\"","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. Pillar 2: The Essential Testimony Guidelines (Key Points to Ingrained in the Client) 2. Overseeing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" When inviting the client in (or hosting a Zoom session) to get them ready, organize the meeting around these three essential pillars: Devote the majority of your time to practicing these three behaviors with the client. Conduct mock questions to assess their understanding and readiness."},"12":{"t":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\"","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. Pillar 3: Enforcement of Dress Code and Professional Demeanor 2. Overseeing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\" When inviting the client in (or hosting a Zoom session) to get them ready, organize the meeting around these three essential pillars: The insurance adjuster will decide how much money to settle a case for based partly on the defense attorney’s \"Evaluation Report\" of the client. The Standard: Advise the client to dress appropriately, as if they were attending a job interview or a funeral— ensuring they appear neat, clean, and respectful. The Warning: Clearly advise them against wearing flashy jewelry, high-end watches, or clothing featuring political or graphic slogans. If they are asserting financial difficulties or significant physical distress, showing up adorned in designer labels undermines their story."},"13":{"t":"Handling the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills)","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Handling the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills) Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. The Strategy: The defense attorney will enumerate their injuries and pose the question, “So, you only hurt your neck—correct?” If the client responds with “Yes,” they are legally prohibited from raising any complaints regarding their back or shoulder later on. Drill A: The “Is That All?” Trap 3. Navigating the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills) During your preparation session, conduct these two targeted mock drills to equip the client with effective defense tactics: The Solution: Instruct the client to respond with, “These are the primary injuries that come to mind at the moment, but my complete medical record thoroughly details everything I’ve discussed with my doctors.”"},"14":{"t":"Handling the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills)","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. The Strategy: The defense attorney will intentionally create a prolonged silence following the client's response, anticipating that the client will start to speak more in order to fill the quiet space. Drill B: The Thoughtful Silence 3. Navigating the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills) During your preparation session, conduct these two targeted mock drills to equip the client with effective defense tactics: The Solution: Advise the client to say, “After you answer the question, make eye contact with the attorney and remain silent. Embrace the awkwardness; that’s their issue, not yours.” Handling the Silent Traps (Case Manager Mock Drills)"},"15":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. Logistics Confirmation: Verify the date, time, and location (or Zoom link) with the client, the attorney, and the court reporter. 4.Case Manager Checklist: The Day Prior to the Deposition Make sure to complete this checklist one day prior to the start of the deposition: Exhibit Preparation: Make certain that all finalized medical records, invoices, and photographs of property damage are organized and readily accessible for your attorney. Emergency Contact Verification: Ensure you have a backup phone number for the client in case they encounter traffic delays or experience technical difficulties on the day of the deposition."},"16":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. What Will the Defense Attorney Ask Me? The defense attorney is allowed to ask a very broad range of questions to discover the facts of the case. Generally, their questions will fall into four main categories:"},"17":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. The 7 Golden Rules for Testifying The defense attorney's primary goal is to get you to contradict yourself, lose your temper, or exaggerate your injuries so they can damage your credibility. Following these seven rules will protect your case: Rule 1: Always Tell the Absolute Truth A damaged case can be saved; a lied-about case cannot. If you lie about a past car accident or a prior back injury, the defense will find out through insurance databases. Once a jury catches a plaintiff in a lie, the case is effectively over. Rule 2: Pause Before You Answer Do not blurt out answers. When a question is asked, pause for two full seconds before speaking. This gives you time to think, ensures the court reporter captures clean audio, and allows your attorney a window to object if the question is unfair. Rule 3: Answer Vocally (No Gestures or Grunts) The court reporter cannot type a head nod, a shrug, or an \"uh-huh.\" You must speak clearly and use verbal words like \"Yes\" or \"No.\""},"18":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. The 7 Golden Rules for Testifying The defense attorney's primary goal is to get you to contradict yourself, lose your temper, or exaggerate your injuries so they can damage your credibility. Following these seven rules will protect your case: Rule 4: Never Guess or Speculate There is a massive difference between estimating and guessing. Good Estimate: \"I was traveling roughly 35 to 40 miles per hour.\" Bad Guess: \"He must have been going 90 miles per hour because of how hard it felt!\" (Unless you looked at his speedometer, you don't actually know). If you do not know an answer, the most powerful and correct phrase you can use is: \"I don't know\" or \"I don't recall.\" Rule 5: Keep Your Answers Short and Concise Answer only the specific question asked, then stop talking. Do not volunteer extra information, and do not try to explain away your answers. If they ask, \"What day of the week did this happen?\", say \"Tuesday.\" Do not say, \"It was a Tuesday, and I remember because I was running late to pick up my daughter from soccer practice...\" Give them a target as small as possible."},"19":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. The 7 Golden Rules for Testifying The defense attorney's primary goal is to get you to contradict yourself, lose your temper, or exaggerate your injuries so they can damage your credibility. Following these seven rules will protect your case: Rule 6: Beware of the \"Silent Trap\" Some defense attorneys will ask a question, listen to your short answer, and then just sit there staring at you in silence. Human nature makes us want to fill awkward silence by rambling. Do not fall for it. If you have answered the question, sit in silence and wait for them to ask the next one. Rule 7: Do Not Try to Win the Case in the Deposition You cannot convince the defense attorney that their driver is a bad person, and you will not get them to apologize. The deposition is simply information gathering. Be polite, stay calm, and leave the legal battling to your attorney."},"20":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. Professional Presentation: Dress Code & Demeanor How you present yourself matters just as much as what you say. The insurance company is using this meeting to judge how a real jury will react to you. What to Wear The Goal: \"Business Casual.\" You want to look respectful, clean, and serious about your legal claim. Men: A collared shirt (button-down or polo), slacks or neat jeans with no holes, and closed-toe shoes. Women: A professional blouse, slacks, a modest dress, or a neat skirt. What to Avoid: Sweatpants, t-shirts with graphic logos, heavy jewelry, hats, or athletic wear. Body Language & Tone Stay Calm: The defense attorney might try to frustrate you. If you get angry, defensive, or sarcastic, they will write down in their report: \"The plaintiff is easily rattled and will look aggressive to a jury.\" Do Not Minimize Your Pain: Be honest about how bad your injuries are, but do not exaggerate either. Use a matter-of-fact, descriptive tone to explain your physical limitations."},"21":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation Preparing Your Client for a Deposition A client's deposition can significantly influence the outcome of a litigated case. As a case manager, you play a crucial role in alleviating client anxiety and ensuring optimal performance. While the attorney focuses on the overarching legal strategy, your responsibility lies in preparing the client both operationally and psychologically. What Happens When Your Lawyer Objects? During the deposition, your attorney may suddenly interrupt and say \"Objection!\" followed by a legal term (like \"Objection, form\" or \"Objection, speculation\"). Do Not Panic: This is standard legal procedure. Your lawyer is placing a formal flag on the record to protect you. Stop Talking Immediately: The moment your attorney speaks, close your mouth. Do not finish your sentence. Listen for Instructions: In 95% of cases, your lawyer will state the objection for the record and then tell you, \"You can answer the question.\" You will then answer it. However, if the question invades your private medical or legal rights, your attorney may say, \"I instruct the witness not to answer.\" If they say that, sit tight and wait for the next question."},"22":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Document Name What It Is Why It Matters to the Case Manager The Complaint / Petition The formal document drafted by your attorney that states the facts of the accident, alleges negligence, and officially demands damages. Kickoff Document: You must track the date this is filed to ensure the Statute of Limitations (SOL) is met. Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The Litigation Document Index You must be able to open a litigation file and instantly identify these five foundational documents. They form the backbone of any lawsuit."},"23":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Document Name What It Is Why It Matters to the Case Manager The Answer The formal response filed by the defense attorney. They will almost always deny liability and list \"affirmative defenses\" (e.g., claiming our client was at fault). The Roadmap: This document tells you exactly what the defense is disputing so you know what evidence you need to hunt for. Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The Litigation Document Index You must be able to open a litigation file and instantly identify these five foundational documents. They form the backbone of any lawsuit."},"24":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Document Name What It Is Why It Matters to the Case Manager Interrogatories Written questions sent from one party to another that must be answered under oath within a strict deadline (usually 30 days). The Deadline Clock: You must schedule the client to help draft these responses immediately upon receipt. Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The Litigation Document Index You must be able to open a litigation file and instantly identify these five foundational documents. They form the backbone of any lawsuit."},"25":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Document Name What It Is Why It Matters to the Case Manager Requests for Production (RFP) A formal demand for physical evidence, including medical records, payroll logs, photos, and social media downloads. Evidence Gathering: You are responsible for compiling every single physical item requested here. Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The Litigation Document Index You must be able to open a litigation file and instantly identify these five foundational documents. They form the backbone of any lawsuit."},"26":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Document Name What It Is Why It Matters to the Case Manager Requests for Admission (RFA) A list of statements the other side must explicitly \"admit\" or \"deny\" (e.g., \"Admit that you were speeding\"). The Danger Zone: If RFAs are not answered within the strict statutory deadline, the court automatically treats them as ADMITTED, which can instantly kill a case. Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The Litigation Document Index You must be able to open a litigation file and instantly identify these five foundational documents. They form the backbone of any lawsuit."},"27":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. Critical Litigation Processes Every Case Manager Must Master Litigation is governed by rigid court procedures. These are the three processes you will execute on a weekly basis: Process 1: The Service of Process (SOP) Pipeline Filing a lawsuit means nothing if the defendant isn't legally notified. You must actively manage the service pipeline to prevent the case from stalling on day one. Process 2: The E-Filing and Docketing System The Rule of the Docket: Every time a document is filed by your attorney or the defense, it generates a \"Docket Entry.\" Your Action: You must check the court docket weekly. Do not rely solely on email notifications. If the defense files a Motion to Dismiss and the notification lands in your spam folder, the clock is still ticking against you."},"28":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. Critical Litigation Processes Every Case Manager Must Master Litigation is governed by rigid court procedures. These are the three processes you will execute on a weekly basis: Process 3: Third-Party Evidence Subpoenas When a hospital, cell phone provider, or employer refuses to hand over records voluntarily, you must initiate the subpoena process. Draft the Subpoena: Fill out the court-approved Subpoena Duces Tecum (a command to produce documents). Notice to Opposing Counsel: Serve a copy to the defense attorney first (most states require a 5-to-10-day notice before serving the third party). Formal Service: Have a process server formally deliver the subpoena to the facility’s Registered Agent. Track Compliance: Call the facility’s legal or records department 5 days before the deadline to ensure the records are being compiled."},"29":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. The 3 Golden Rules of Litigation File Maintenance Rule 1: Separate Pre-Lit from Lit The moment a lawsuit is filed, cleanly separate your file structure. Create explicit sub-folders for: Pleadings, Discovery (Sent & Received), Motions, and Subpoenas. Mixing pre-lit correspondence with court pleadings creates operational chaos. Rule 2: Read the \"Scheduling Order\" Like the Bible Early in the case, the judge will issue a Scheduling Order listing absolute deadlines for discovery cutoff, expert witness designations, and motion filings. Print this out, put it on the front of the file, and input every single date into the firm's master calendar with 30-day, 14-day, and 7-day warning alerts. Rule 3: Document All Extensions in Writing If a defense attorney asks for an extra week to answer discovery, and your attorney agrees, get it in writing via email. A verbal agreement over the phone does not exist in the eyes of the court. Send a brief confirmation email: \"Per our phone call, we agree to extend your discovery deadline to [Date].\""},"30":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Litigation Toolkit – Core Documents & Critical Processes When litigation begins, documents transition from \"administrative tracking\" to evidence. Missing, misfiled, or disorganized documents can jeopardize a trial. As a litigation case manager, it's essential to understand the necessary documents and processes to manage a case from filing to trial effectively. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) for Document Management The 48-Hour Docket Rule: Any document received from the court or opposing counsel must be filed into the internal system and docketed on the calendar within 48 hours. RFA Priority Check: Treat Requests for Admission as a structural fire. Alert the assigned attorney the exact hour they land on your desk. Subpoena Audit: IReview all outstanding subpoenas every 10 days to ensure third-party compliance."},"31":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Deep-Dive: Document Anatomy & Checklists The Complaint / Petition The Complaint formally initiates the lawsuit. While the attorney dictates the legal arguments, the case manager is responsible for verifying the factual foundation. Case Manager Verification Checklist: Before submitting a Complaint to your attorney for signature, you must check three things: [ ] The \"Statute Date\": Is the filing date safely before the Statute of Limitations? [ ] Entity Verification: Did you verify the defendant’s legal name via the Secretary of State or property records? (e.g., Is it John Doe, or John Doe Enterprises, LLC?) [ ] The Registered Agent: If the defendant is a business, did you locate the exact name and address of their Registered Agent for service?"},"32":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Written Discovery: Interrogatories, RFPs, and RFAs When the defense sends a discovery packet, it must be dissected and calendar- tracked immediately. Drafting Standard Interrogatories (To Defendant): When sending our own questions to the defense, always ensure you include baseline questions regarding: Insurance Coverage: Request all primary, excess, and umbrella policy limits. Course and Scope: Ask if the defendant driver was working or running an errand for an employer at the exact moment of the crash. Surveillance: Formally ask if they have hired a private investigator to follow or film our client."},"33":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Master Timeline & Deadline Calculations In litigation, missing a deadline by one day can cause a case to be dismissed with prejudice (meaning it can never be refiled). You must master how to calculate legal time. The Federal / State Calculation Rule Most jurisdictions follow a standard framework for counting days: Exclude the day of the event that triggers the period, and include the last day of the period. The \"Mail Box\" Trap: If a document is served via mail or electronic portal instead of hand-delivery, many states automatically add 3 additional days to your response timeline. You must confirm your local civil procedure rules regarding electronic service extensions."},"34":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. The Process Server & Evading Defendant Protocol When a defendant realizes they are being sued, they frequently hide, refuse to answer the door, or quit their jobs to evade service. If you do not serve them before the court's deadline, the judge will dismiss the file for Want of Prosecution."},"35":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Subpoena Duces Tecum: Execution Strategy A Subpoena Duces Tecum is a powerful tool to force hostile or uncooperative third parties to turn over evidence. The Scope Warning: Never send a blanket subpoena asking for \"all records of any kind.\" It will trigger a Motion to Quash from the defense or third party, claiming it is an overly broad fishing expedition. Keep it specific: \"Any and all emergency room records, triage notes, and radiological imaging regarding [Client] for the treatment date of [Accident Date] only.\""},"36":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Litigation File Architecture To keep files uniform across the entire firm, every digital file must match this sub-folder structure the moment a lawsuit is filed:"},"37":{"t":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition","s":"Case Phases : Litigation","w":"Case Manager Checklist: The Day Before the Deposition Case Phases : Litigation The Advanced Litigation Workflow & Document Deep-Dive To excel as a litigation case manager, one must understand not just document names, but their structure, drafting components, and relevant legal timelines. This module details the blueprints, templates, and timeline logistics necessary for managing a high- volume litigation docket accurately. Advanced KPIs for Litigation Case Managers [ ] The Zero-Default Policy: Never let a discovery or motion deadline pass without a filed response or a written, signed extension agreement from opposing counsel. [ ] The 14-Day Service Milestone: If a process server has not successfully served a defendant within 14 days of receiving the packet, an interactive skip-trace must be conducted and a status update sent to the attorney. [ ] The \"Clean\" Discovery Check: Before handing a drafted discovery response to your attorney, verify that every blank space is filled and a signature-ready verification page is attached."},"38":{"t":"Case Overview Objective:","s":"Skill Building : The Ultimate Case Management","w":"Skill Building : The Ultimate Case Management Case Overview Client Name: Jordan Davies Date of Accident (DOA): March 12, 2026 Case Type: Motor Vehicle Accident (MVA) – Personal Injury Property Damage: Total Loss (Vehicle towed from the scene) Status: Pre-Litigation / Active Treatment Key Issues: Liability Dispute (Defendant claims Jordan ran a yellow/red light), Client Refusing Treatment. Objective: The Jordan Davies file aims to assess your critical thinking, client communication, and investigative abilities. You will evaluate a pre- litigation file with high medical bills, low coverage, a liability dispute, and a concerned client. Review all documents, create the file in the Training Interface, and complete the required phases. Phase 1: Your client has $50,000 in medical bills and is worried because the at-fault driver only has a $10,000 Bodily Injury (BI) policy. Your Task: Review all provided Declarations (Dec) Pages carefully. Goal: Identify the total actual available coverage. Look beyond the obvious policies. Remember to check household addresses and resident relative rules to find any hidden Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist (UM/UIM) coverage that can be stacked or utilized to save this case. Phase 2: Jordan has stopped going to Physical Therapy because he is terrified of drowning in medical debt, creating a dangerous \"gap in treatment.\" Your Task: Roleplay a phone call with Jordan to get him back into treatment. Goal: Validate his financial fears, but clearly explain how a Letter of Protection (Medical Lien) works. You must educate him on why stopping treatment again"},"39":{"t":"Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation.","s":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation","w":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. When someone's negligence or intentional act harms your property—whether it is real estate (like a house) or personal property (like a car)—the law provides a mechanism to try and make you \"whole\" again financially. 1. Types of Damages You Can Recover In property damage litigation, the goal is not to give the plaintiff a windfall, but to restore them to the financial position they were in before the incident occurred."},"40":{"t":"Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation.","s":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation","w":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. When someone's negligence or intentional act harms your property—whether it is real estate (like a house) or personal property (like a car)—the law provides a mechanism to try and make you \"whole\" again financially. 2. Calculating the Value of the Claim One of the most heavily litigated aspects of a property damage case is calculating exactly how much the defendant owes. Courts generally apply specific formulas to determine this: The \"Lesser Of\" Rule: For personal property, courts typically award the lesser of two amounts: the cost to repair the item, or the \"diminution of value\" (the item's fair market value before the damage minus its value after). For example, if a car is worth $5,000 but repairs cost $7,000, the court will likely cap the damages at $5,000 (totaling the vehicle)."},"41":{"t":"Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation.","s":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation","w":"Property Damage and How it affects a case in Litigation Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. Property damage is a cornerstone of civil litigation. When someone's negligence or intentional act harms your property—whether it is real estate (like a house) or personal property (like a car)—the law provides a mechanism to try and make you \"whole\" again financially. 2. Calculating the Value of the Claim One of the most heavily litigated aspects of a property damage case is calculating exactly how much the defendant owes. Courts generally apply specific formulas to determine this: Sentimental or Special Value: Standard law focuses strictly on Fair Market Value. However, if an item has a unique, peculiar value to the owner, some jurisdictions allow for additional recovery, though this requires proving the defendant had notice of the unique value before the incident or acted intentionally."},"42":{"t":"Thank You!","s":"Thank You!","w":"Thank You!"}},"open":[1,2],"close":[42],"topics":[{"h":"The Case Manager's Tactical Role in Litigation","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[3]},{"h":"Phase-by-Phase Core Responsibilities","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[4,5,6,7]},{"h":"The Litigation Mindset: Critical Traps to Avoid","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[8]},{"h":"The Pre-Deposition Audit","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[9]},{"h":"Managing the \"Discovery Intake Meeting\"","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[10,11,12]},{"h":"Handling the Silent Traps: Mock Drills","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[13,14]},{"h":"Preparing Your Client for a Deposition","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[15,16,17,18,19,20,21]},{"h":"The Litigation Toolkit: Core Documents and Critical Processes","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[22,23,24,25,26,27,28,29,30]},{"h":"The Advanced Litigation Workflow and Document Deep-Dive","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[31,32,33,34,35,36,37]},{"h":"Skill Building: the Ultimate Case Management Challenge","section":"Case Phase: Litigation","pages":[38]},{"h":"Property Damage and How It Affects a Case in Litigation","section":"Property Damage","pages":[39,40,41]}],"w":1920,"h":1080}};
